Cell[BoxData["\<\"2107.06696\"\>"], "Echo",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9311485123622093`*^9},
 CellID->17259409,ExpressionUUID->"501b861c-00b8-47e4-8fc1-b41721315628"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TabViewBox[{{1,
  "1"->"\<\"\\\\begin{document}\\n\\\\maketitle\\n\\n\\\\begin{abstract} The \
spontaneous formation and subsequent growth, dissolution, merger and \
competition of social groups bears similarities to physical phase transitions \
in metastable finite systems.\\n  We examine three different scenarios, \
percolation, spinodal decomposition and nucleation, to describe the formation \
of social groups of varying size and density. \\n  In our agent-based model, \
we use a feedback between the opinions of agents and their ability to \
establish links.\\n  Groups can restrict further link formation, but agents \
can also leave if costs exceed the group benefits.\\n  We identify the \
critical parameters for costs/benefits and social influence to obtain either \
one large group or the stable coexistence of several groups with different \
opinions.\\n  Analytic investigations allow to derive different critical \
densities \\n  that control the formation and coexistence of groups.\\nOur \
novel approach sheds new light on the early stage of network growth and the \
emergence of large connected components.     \\n\\n\\n\\\\end{abstract}\\n\\n\
\\n\\n\\n\\n\"\>"}, {2,
  "2"->"\<\"{Introduction}\\n\\\\label{sec:introduction}\\n\\nModels of \
social systems frequently utilize the network approach where\\nnodes \
represent individuals, or agents in general, while links represent social \
interactions between individuals.\\nThis approach is widely used not only in \
social physics \
\\\\citep{weidlich-book-00,castellano2009,Schweitzer2018,BATTISTON20201}, but \
also in the social sciences which have established their own tradition to \
study social networks already back in the 1940s \
\\\\citep{scott1988social,prell2012social}.\\nIt advocates a \
\\\\emph{structural} perspective where nodes can be characterized based on \
the topological properties of the network.\\nThe different centrality \
measures to quantify the importance of nodes are good examples to demonstrate \
the success of this approach \
\\\\citep{friedkin1991theoretical,everett2005extending,bonacich}.\\nAt the \
same time, they also illustrate the limitations of the structural \
perspective.\\nThe temporal sequence of interactions is neglected \
\\\\citep{Scholtes2016,falzon2018embedding}, despite the fact that the bursty \
nature of the social dynamics is important \
\\\\citep{Karsai-Bursty}.\\nFurther, the internal dynamics of nodes play no \
role \\\\citep{macy2002factors,Schweitzer2020}.\\nIf agents represent social \
individuals, internal degrees of freedom cannot be ignored \
\\\\citep{bonabeau2002agent,M_s_2013,macy2002factors}.\\nIndividuals make \
decisions, for example to whom to establish a relation or when to leave a \
group, they consider costs and benefits before joining, e.g., an online \
social network.\\nHence, links between individuals are not primarily \
established by chance, but by choice.\\nThe \\\\emph{Why} matters as much as \
the \\\\emph{How}.\\n\\nThese issues become of importance when the \
\\\\emph{formation} of networks shall be explained.\\nEstablished model \
classes from the social sciences, for instance exponential random graph \
models (ERGM) \\\\citep{lusher2013exponential,cranmer2011inferential}, aim at \
including social mechanisms such as homophily, reciprocity  or triadic \
closure \\\\citep{currarini2016simple,gorski2020homophily,block2019forms,\
Schweitzer2019} to explain the formation of social links.\\nThe problems of \
these models to cope with repeated interactions, the temporal order of \
interactions, or simply with a large number of agents, cannot be discussed \
here \\\\citep{leifeld2018temporal,krivitsky2011adjusting}.\\n\\nBut models \
proposed in the context of statistical physics or random graph theory do not \
fare better.\\nThey mostly feature even simpler mechanisms of random link \
formation,\\nlike the random graph models from the 1950s \
\\\\citep{erdos_1960,gilbert_1959} or the small world network model \
\\\\citep{watts1998collective}. \\nThe probability of link formation can be \
also biased to take into account, e.g. the preferential attachment to nodes \
with a high degree or a high (static) ``fitness'' \
\\\\citep{golosovsky2018mechanisms,musmeci2013bootstrapping}, or to nodes \
with similar degree (assortativity) \
\\\\citep{noldus2015assortativity,catanzaro2004social}.\\nSuch models have \
the problem, in addition to the \\\\emph{ad hoc} motivation of the attachment \
rules, that they necessarily lead to a largely \\\\emph{connected} network \
characterized by a certain degree distribution.\\nThat is understandable \
because their focus is on the \\\\emph{result}\\nrather than the \
\\\\emph{process} of network formation.\\n\\nOur aim is to change this focus \
toward the early stages of network growth.\\nTo adequately capture this \
dynamics, we develop a new perspective that combines processes of social \
group formation with physical models of phase transitions.\\nOur approach \
considers that large connected social systems can emerge from  different \
mechanisms \
\\\\citep{Fronczak2007,rivera2010dynamics,PODOBNIK2019613,Capocci2006,\
arenas2008motif,Singh2016}.\\nOnline social networks are the most studied \
ones \\\\citep{musial2013creation,bielenberg2012growth,mislove2008growth,\
garcia2013b,Capocci2006}.\\nThey grow when new users join and link to \
established users .\\nThe costs involved are low, therefore we \\nobserve a \
high entry rate, and users create many links.\\nThis often results in a large \
connected component and a core-periphery structure of the social network \
\\\\citep{BATTISTON20201,Garc_a_Mu_iz_2006,Gamble_2015}.\\nDisconnected \
components also exist, but they are comparably small and only contain a \
negligible fraction of users.\\n\\nIn the offline social world it is more \
costly to establish links.\\nIt is already costly to find the right partners, \
relations need to be maintained with more effort and will be discarded if not \
beneficial \\\\citep{Schweitzer2019c,Collins2018}.\\nHence, instead of one \
large connected network, we find a number of different groups coexisting \
\\\\citep{Onnela2011,Brodka2013}.\\nIndividuals are densely connected within, \
but not necessarily across groups \
\\\\citep{granovetter_1973,BATTISTON20201,bianconi2014triadic}.\\nIndividuals \
can leave a group to join another one, groups can also merge if they find \
that they have enough in common.\\nFurther, groups can polarize \
\\\\citep{macy2003polarization,flache2017,schweighofer_2020_2,schweighofer_\
2020} because they represent different cultures or opinions, they can compete \
for members that have to be convinced to join \
\\\\citep{Boyd2009,M_s_2013,groeber_2009_2}.\\n\\nIn order to model these \
processes of group formation from a unifying perspective, we need to consider \
a number of social ``ingredients'', which are explained in more detail in \
Section~\\\\ref{sec:basics}.\\nAgents in our model have to overcome an entry \
barrier if they want to form a new group or join an existing one.\\nThey \
should consider costs and benefits of belonging to a group \
\\\\citep{Wilkins2010,Schweitzer2019c}. \\nGroups can influence their members \
and can build up a group identity. \\nBased on this, groups should have the \
ability to restrict the admission of agents.\\nIn addition to the formation \
of one large group, we should also allow for the stable coexistence of \
different groups, even for competition between groups.\\n\\nTo achieve such a \
unifying perspective, we build on one central feedback.\\nIn our model, \
agents are characterized by an internal continuous variable, generally \
speaking an ``opinion'', which can change over time.\\nTheir opinion \
determines the possibility to establish links to other agents, this way \
forming a group.\\nThe group, on the other hand, influences the ability of \
its members to create further links.\\nHence, there is a feedback between \
\\\\emph{opinion dynamics} and \\\\emph{group formation}.\\nWith additional \
assumptions about socially motivated costs and benefits and about the \
boundary conditions for network formation we are able to develop a large \
variety of group structures as demonstrated in \
Section~\\\\ref{sec:clust-form-netw}. \\n\\nThe physical models of phase \
transitions come into play when we try to distinguish \\\\emph{three \
different scenarios} of group formation and their ability to establish one \
large group.\\nFor this classification, we develop analogies between group \
formation \\nof individuals and phase transitions in metastable and unstable \
thermodynamic systems.\\nThe spontaneous formation of a new phase under \
supercritical conditions is known from spinodal decomposition \
\\\\citep{cahn1965phase,langer1971theory} and from  percolation in porous \
media \\\\citep{Stauffer_1979}.\\nNucleation processes, on the other hand, \
first lead to a larger number of clusters of subcritical size and only a few \
may spontaneously grow, to form the new thermodynamic phase \
\\\\citep{abraham1974homogeneous, Book-Ulbricht1988,oxtoby1992homogeneous, \
schmelzer2005nucleation}.  \\n\\nFrom these three scenarios, so far only \
percolation has been discussed in the context of network formation \
\\\\citep{Shao2009, Allard2018,Eguiluz2003,solomon-et-00, Callaway2000, \
schweitzer_2021}.\\nWhen gradually adding links and nodes to a network, \
\\npercolation describes in the emergence of a giant connected component, \
which resembles a second-order phase transition.\\nRecently, models for \
explosive percolation have been proposed that allow for new universality \
classes in the characterization of such phase transitions.\\nThese models \
introduce new mechanisms, such as the product rule \
\\\\citep{achlioptas_2009}, to influence the type of phase transitions.\\nBut \
nucleation or spinodal decomposition as established mechanisms of \
thermodynamic phase transitions have not been considered to describe the \
formation of networks.\\n\\nWe will close this gap in our paper, which is \
organized as follows:\\nIn Section~\\\\ref{sec:basics}, we recap some basics \
of thermodynamic phase transitions, to provide the concepts later used in the \
paper.\\nThe main part of this section is devoted to introduce, and to \
formalize, the social components of our model.\\nWe will also demonstrate how \
the restrictions for agents to form links impact the percolation threshold as \
one measure of a phase transition.\\n\\nIn \
Section~\\\\ref{sec:clust-form-netw}, we introduce a stochastic approach to \
group formation by motivating transition rates for different processes of \
growth and dissolution of groups.\\nWe then present a large number of \
agent-based computer simulations to illustrate the three different scenarios.\
\\nEventually, we provide a systematic study of the parameter space, to \
distinguish the three scenarios.\\n\\nSection~\\\\ref{sec:analyt-invest} \
presents analytical investigations of our model and corresponding computer \
simulations, to fully understand the growth, dissolution, competition and \
coexistence of groups.\\nBy analyzing two limit cases, incremental growth and \
densification, we are able to derive formal expressions for critical \
densities that capture the essential differences in the dynamics of \
groups.\\n\\nIn Section~\\\\ref{sec:discussion-1}, we spend effort to \
assemble the various analytical and simulation results into a comprehensive \
and coherent view of group formation as a phase transition.\\nWe then link \
the discussion back to our starting point, by examining the relevance of our \
results for modeling social systems.\\nEventually, we provide insights of how \
our modeling approach shall be applied and extended toward multidimensional \
opinion dynamics and multi-layer networks.\\nIt manifests that our modeling \
approach sheds new lights on social processes of group formation, and has the \
potential to open new routes \\nto study the dynamics of social \
networks.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\\
n\\n\"\>"}, {3,
  "3"->"\<\"{From physical to social models}\\n\\\\label{sec:basics}\\n\\n\\n\
\\\\subsection{Kinetics of phase \
transitions}\\n\\\\label{sec:mech-phase-trans}\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{Control \
parameter. \\\\ }\\n\\nAs a reference point for our discussions, we shortly \
summarize kinetic aspects of phase transitions in thermodynamic systems \
\\\\citep{Book-Ulbricht1988,oxtoby1992homogeneous,schmelzer2005nucleation}.\\\
nConsider a finite system in a gaseous state with the boundary conditions \
$N,V,T$ = const.\\n$N$ is the number of molecules in a vapor, for example, \
$V$ is the system volume and $T$ is the temperature.\\nThe corresponding \
thermodynamic potential is the free energy $F(N,V,T)$.\\nFor fixed $N$, $V$ \
the temperature determines the stability of the gaseous \
system.\\nSpecifically, we can define a density $\\\\rho=N/V$ and an \
equilibrium density $\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{eq}}(T)$.\\nThen the control \
parameter $y=\\\\rho/\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{eq}}(T)$, known as \
\\\\emph{supersaturation}, describes whether the system is in an equilibrium, \
$y=1$ or an unstable state, i.e. $y\\\\gg 1$.\\nIn the  supersaturated case, \
we can observe a phase transition, for instance the formation of small water \
droplets in a water vapor.\\nWhether a macroscopic liquid phase is formed, \
crucially depends on the size of the system and the value of the \
supersaturation \\\\citep{09-fs-lsg-87}.\\nSystem size matters because of the \
conservation of molecules.\\nIf droplets are formed, we have a thermodynamic \
system with two phases, the gaseous phase $(\\\\beta)$ and the liquid phase \
$(\\\\alpha)$.\\nHence\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:30}\\n  \
N=&N_{0}(t)+\\\\sum_{k=1}^{K}n_{k}(t) N_{k}(t)\\n\\\\end{align}\\n\\nwhere \
$N_{0}$ is the number of molecules in the gaseous phase, while the sum \
contains the number of molecules in the liquid phase. \\n$n_{k}$ is the size \
of a droplet, given by the number of molecules, and $N_{k}$ is the number of \
droplets with size $n_{k}$.\\nThus, once droplets are formed, the initial \
supersaturation $y$ decreases, and we have instead the \\\\emph{actual} \
supersaturation \
$y_{0}(t)=N_{0}(t)/[V\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{eq}}(T)]$.\\n\\nAdditionally, there \
are energetic considerations.\\nAssume spherical droplets with a radius \
$r_{k}=[3n_{k}/(4\\\\pi\\\\rho_{\\\\alpha})]^{1/3}$, where \
$\\\\rho_{\\\\alpha}$ is the density of the liquid phase.\\nThese droplets \
are characterized by a surface tension $\\\\sigma$.\\nThe formation of this \
surface requires the Gibbs surface energy $W=(4\\\\pi\\\\sigma/3)r_{k}^{2}$, \
while the formation of the bulk phase releases energy \
\\\\citep{schmelzer2006classical}.\\nThis results in a critical droplet \
radius\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:31}\\n  \
r^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}=\\\\frac{2\\\\sigma}{C}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\ln \
y}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nwhere $C$ is a dimensionality constant that contains \
the density $\\\\rho_{\\\\alpha}$ and the temperature. \\nDroplets that have \
reached the critical size will grow further until the free energy has reached \
its minimum. \\nBut to obtain the critical size requires to overcome the \
energy barrier characterized by a maximum of the free energy and needs to \
consider fluctuations \
\\\\citep{abraham1974homogeneous,17-fs-88}.\\n\\\\paragraph{Three scenarios \
of phase transitions. \\\\ }\\n\\n\\nEqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:31} already points to \
the conditions under which phase transitions occur.\\nWe can use these \
conditions to distinguish between three different scenarios. \\nFirstly, we \
can have a very large initial supersaturation $y$, which characterizes an \
unstable system.\\nThen the energy barrier is negligible and the macroscopic \
liquid phase forms immediately, surrounded by the saturated gaseous phase. \
\\nThis is known as \\\\emph{spinodal decomposition} \
\\\\citep{cahn1965phase,langer1971theory}.\\n\\nSecondly, we can have a \
medium initial supersaturation, but a negligible\\nvalue of the surface \
tension $\\\\sigma$.\\nThen, again, the energy barrier is negligible, and a \
macroscopic liquid phase forms until the system saturates.\\nThis dynamics \
has analogies to \\\\emph{percolation}, where links are formed between \
occupied lattice sites \\\\citep{Stauffer_1979}.\\nIt is known that for a 2d \
regular lattice of size $N$ the critical density is \
$\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}=n_{c}/N=0.593$, i.e. if $n_{c}$ lattice sites are \
occupied, we can expect to find a percolating cluster in the limit of large \
$N$, which is the equivalent of a macroscopic phase. \\nThere is no surface \
tension involved in the formation of clusters.\\n\\nThe third scenario, \
\\\\emph{nucleation}, is the most interesting one.\\nIt is characterized by a \
medium initial supersaturation and a non-negligible surface tension.\\nThis \
implies a rather large energy barrier and thus a large critical \
radius.\\nHence, the system is initially in a \\\\emph{metastable} \
state.\\nThis results in the formation of a larger number of small  droplets \
of subcritical size.\\nDependent on the supersaturation, a fraction of these \
droplets can reach a supercritical size and grow further.\\nThis reduces the \
actual supersaturation drastically and no new droplets can form.\\nBut the \
supercritical droplets still have to form a macroscopic phase.\\nThis dynamic \
process is known as Ostwald ripening \
\\\\citep{voorhees1985theory,marqusee1983kinetics,12-schmelzer-fs-87-b,Book-\
Ulbricht1988}.\\nEstablished droplets can grow further only \
if:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:32}\\n  \\\\frac{dr_{k}(t)}{dt}& \
=\\\\frac{2\\\\sigma}{C} \
\\\\left[\\\\frac{1}{r_{0}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)}-\\\\frac{1}{r_{k}(t)}\\\\\
right]\\n\\\\end{align}\\nHere, $r_{0}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)={2\\\\sigma}/{[C\\\
\\ln y_{0}(t)]}$ is the actual critical radius that depends on the actual \
supersaturation $y_{0}(t)$.\\nDroplets with a subcritical radius shrink via \
re-evaporation, which allows droplets with a supercritical radius to further \
grow. \\nAdditionally, processes of \\\\emph{coagulation} can be considered \
which  happen if droplets of different size collide.\\nAlso processes of \
\\\\emph{fragmentation} can occur, but for droplets they are \
negligible.\\n\\n\\n\\\\subsection{Group \
formation}\\n\\\\label{sec:mech-group-form}\\n\\nHow can we link the three \
different scenarios for phase transitions summarized above to the formation \
of social groups?\\nJust renaming droplets as groups will not lead to any new \
insights,\\nbut would be also wrong.\\nWe shortly discuss the main \
differences in the following.\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{Groups with varying density. \
\\\\ }\\n\\nFirstly, social groups are not spherical clusters with radius \
$r_{k}$, equal bulk density $\\\\rho_{\\\\alpha}$ and equal surface tension $\
\\\\sigma$.\\nThey are rather like small \\\\emph{networks} (see \
Figure~\\\\ref{fig:symbolic representation}), characterized by the number of \
group members  which we call \\\\emph{agents} in the following, and the \
number of links that connect them.\\nConnected agents form a \\\\emph{group} \
$g_{k}[n_{k},m_{k}]$, where $k$ is the group \\\\emph{index}, $n_{k}$ is the \
number of agents and $m_{k}$ is the number of links in group $k$.\\n$K$ then \
denotes the \\\\emph{total number} of groups, which can change over time. \\n\
\\nWe further define a group density $\\\\rho_{k}=2m_{k}/n_{k}$.\\nIt  is not \
a constant either, but can change by agents joining or leaving or by adding \
or removing social links.\\nThe factor of 2 reflects that each link connects \
two agents, so we normalize the number of links to $n_{k}/2$. \\nNote that \
$\\\\rho_{k}$ is normalized to the \\\\emph{size} of the group rather than to \
the number of possible links, $n_{k}(n_{k}-1)$,  as used in network science. \
\\nIndeed, our definition is akin to the average degree of an agent in the \
group.\\n\\nThe minimum number of links in a group is \
$m_{k}^{\\\\mathrm{min}}=(n_{k}-1)\\\\simeq n_{k}$, i.e. each agent is \
connected via only one link, for example in a star-like topology.\\nHence $\\\
\\rho_{k}=2n_{k}/(n_{k}-1)\\\\simeq 2$  if only \\\\textit{incremental \
growth} is considered, i.e. groups grow by incorporating one new agent at a \
time through the formation of a single link.\\nNote that $\\\\rho_{k}=2$ is \
the limiting density for large groups in the case of incremental \
growth.\\nSmall groups always start from two agents with one link, i.e. \
$\\\\rho_{k}=1$.\\n\\nOn the other hand, the maximum number of (undirected) \
links in a group is $m_{k}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}=n_{k}(n_{k}-1)/2\\\\simeq \
n_{k}^{2}/2$, i.e. agents form a fully connected network.\\nThen \
$\\\\rho_{k}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}=(n_{k}-1)\\\\simeq n_{k}$ is the maximum \
density\\nif groups are allowed to densify, i.e. agents create links within a \
group. \\nWith these considerations it holds that  $\\\\rho_{k}\\\\in \
[1,\\\\{2, n_{k}\\\\}]$.\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{Group utility. \\\\ }\\n\\nTo \
further distinguish social groups from droplets, we characterize groups of \
different $n_{k}$, $m_{k}$ by means of a \
\\\\emph{utility}:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:88}\\n  \
u_{k}[n_{k},m_{k}] = b m_{k} - c n_{k}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nIt reflects the \
social insight that being part of a group has benefits and costs.\\nThe \
benefits $bm_{k}$ arise from the existence of links within a group.\\nMore \
links allow more interactions, a better exchange of information, etc., as \
argued in management science \\\\citep{Uzzi-network-effect}.\\nThis differs \
from definitions of utilities in, e.g., economics that assign maintenance \
\\\\emph{costs} to links \\\\citep{Battiston2012}.\\nOn the other hand, \
maintaining a group is costly, therefore \\n$cn_{k}$ denotes a cost \
proportional to the number of agents that need to be integrated into the \
group.\\nThe linear form for the utility function is chosen because of its \
minimal assumptions.\\nWe have no evidence for suitable nonlinear \
forms.\\nFurther, we do not make the utility dependent on local substructures \
of the group that differ across agents, but on the group as a whole. \
\\n\\nOnce groups are established, agents will consider costs and benefits \
from being part of a group and will leave if not satisfied, as explained \
later in Sect.~\\\\ref{sec:transition-rates}.\\n\\nThe \\\\emph{maximum} \
utility is $u_{k}^{\\\\mathrm{max}}=(b/2)\\\\, n_{k}^{2} - c n_{k}$.\\nThe \\\
\\emph{minimum} utility, on the other hand, is \
$u_{k}^{\\\\mathrm{min}}=(b-c)n_{k}$.\\nThus, if $(b-c)<0$, \
$u(n_{k},m_{k})=0$ defines the minimum size of a group to have a positive \
utility, namely $\\\\hat{n}_{k}=(b/c)m_{k}$.\\nUsing the group density \
$\\\\rho_{k}=2m_{k}/n_{k}$, \\nthe respective density is then: \
$\\\\hat{\\\\rho}=2c/b$.\\nThis allows us to rewrite the utility \
as\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:24}\\n  u_{k}(n_{k},\\\\rho_{k}) = c\\\
\\, n_{k}\\\\left[\\\\frac{\\\\rho_{k}}{\\\\hat{\\\\rho}}-1 \
\\\\right]\\n\\\\end{align}\\nAs we will show below, \\ngroups with \
$\\\\rho_{k}> \\\\hat{\\\\rho}$ likely remain or even grow, while groups with \
$\\\\rho_{k}< \\\\hat{\\\\rho}$ likely \
dissolve.\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{Homophily. \\\\ }\\n\\nIn addition to the \
varying densities $\\\\rho_{k}$ and utilities $u_{k}$, there are dynamic \
peculiarities that distinguish social groups from droplets.\\nThe social \
principle of \\\\emph{homophily} \
\\\\citep{mcpherson2001birds,mcmillan2022worth,gorski2020homophily,\
currarini2016simple} states that agents tend to interact more often with \
those others that are similar in some respect.\\nTo formalize this, we \
characterize agents by a \\\\emph{scalar variable} $x_{i}$, drawn from a \
uniform distribution $U(0,1)$.\\n$x_{i}(t)$ could represent, in a very \
general sense, the \\\\emph{opinion} of agent $i$ with respect to some issue, \
which can change over time as will be discussed below.\\nThe uniform \
distribution of initial opinions is chosen because it requires the least \
constraints and because it  allows to consider more extreme opinions, which \
are likely to be underrepresented by a normal distribution.\\n\\nTwo randomly \
chosen agents $i$ and $j$ will successfully interact only if the difference \
in their $x$ values is below a certain \\\\emph{threshold} $\\\\epsilon$, \
which is also interpreted as a \\\\emph{tolerance} for deviating \
opinions.\\nTo formalize this, we introduce a variable:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n \
 \\\\label{eq:19}\\n  z_{ij}(t)= \\\\epsilon - \
\\\\abs{x_{j}(t)-x_{i}(t)}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nAgents successfully interact \
only if $z\\\\geq 0$.\\nTheir interaction triggers another feedback, \
\\\\emph{convergence}: Because of \\\\emph{social influence} \
\\\\citep{mavrodiev2013quantifying,flache2017,groeber_2014,flache2011local} \
agents' opinions tend to become more similar if they interact more \
\\\\citep{Helbing2011crowd, mavrodiev_2021}.\\nTo include a dynamics for \
$x_{i}(t)$, we adopt for simplicity the bounded confidence model \
\\\\citep{deffuant_2000,hegselmann_2002,lorenz_2007,groeber_2009_2}.\\nA more \
complex opinion dynamics for multidimensional opinions can be considered as \
well \\\\citep{schweighofer_2020,schweighofer_2020_2}.\\nTwo randomly chosen \
agents $i$ and $j$ update their opinions $x_{i}$, $x_{i}$ \\\\emph{if} they \
interact as follows:\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n  \\\\label{eq:mean}\\n  \
\\\\frac{\\\\Delta x_{i}(t)}{\\\\Delta t}= \\\\gamma \\\\left[x_{j}(t) - \
x_{i}(t)\\\\right] \\\\Theta[z_{ij}(t)]\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nHere \
$\\\\mathrm{\\\\Theta}[z]$ is the Heaviside function, which returns 0 if \
$z<0$ and 1 if $z\\\\geq 0$. \\nIf we choose the maximum value \
$\\\\gamma=0.5$, there is convergence to the mean $(x_{i}+x_{j})/2$ in one \
time step.\\nSmaller values of $\\\\gamma$ require more interactions to \
obtain more similar opinions. \\nWe note that the dynamics of \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:mean} together with a random sequential update of agents' \
opinions is \\\\emph{path dependent}, i.e. the sequence of interactions \
matters for determining the final opinion of an agent. \
\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{Group formation and group influence. \\\\ }\\n\\nOur main \
assumption is that group formation builds on successful interactions.\\nThat \
means, if  two randomly chosen agents $i$ and $j$ are able to interact \
because their opinions are similar enough, $z_{ij}>0$, they will also form a \
link, $a_{ij}=1$: \\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \
\\\\label{eq:19a}\\na_{ij}(t)=\\\\mathrm{\\\\Theta}[z_{ij}]\\n\\\\end{align}\\\
nThis is the central mechanism for the formation of groups:\\nwhile \
interactions can happen randomly, \\nthe formation of links depends on \
similarity, i.e. the partner is not randomly chosen.\\nThis considers that  \
social interactions are costly.\\nWe emphasize that the existence of a link \
indicates a \\\\emph{special relation} between agents, which is to be \
distinguished from a mere interaction. \\nTherefore, once agents formed a \
group with other agents of similar opinions, they tend to keep these \
relations.   \\nThis implies that groups continue to have an influence on the \
opinion of their members \\\\citep{holyst2001social}, which is captured  in \
an \\\\emph{effective opinion} \
\\\\citep{groeber_2009_2}:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\nx_i^{\\\\textrm{eff}}(t) =& \
\\\\left[1-\\\\alpha\\\\right]x_i(t) + \\\\alpha \\\\mean{x_i^k(t)}\\n        \
           \\\\label{eq:13}\\n\\\\end{align}\\n$ \
\\\\mean{x_i^k(t)}=\\\\sum_{j\\\\in \\\\abs{n_{k}}} x_{j}(t)/n_{k}$\\nis the \
average opinion of group $k$, and $\\\\alpha$ weights its influence on agent \
$i$.\\nConsidering the group influence, we can modify $z_{ij}$ in \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:19} as follows: \\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:33}\\n \
 z_{ij}(t)= \\\\epsilon - \
\\\\abs{x^{\\\\mathrm{eff}}_{j}(t)-x^{\\\\mathrm{eff}}_{i}(t)}\\n\\\\end{\
align}\\nThat means, instead of the individual opinion of an agent, its \
weighted opinion determines whether a link is formed or deleted.\\nIn the \
limit $\\\\alpha=1$ the effective opinion for everyone in the group is the \
same.\\n\\nWe emphasize that group influence reflects another social \
mechanism, \\\\emph{triadic closure} \
\\\\citep{klimek2013triadic,bianconi2014triadic,Schweitzer2019}.\\nOpen \
triads refer to three agents $i$, $j$ and $k$ which are connected by only two \
links $(i,j)$ and $(j,k)$.\\nThen the probability that either $i$ or $k$ form \
the third link $(i,k)$  to close the triad is much larger than the \
probability that either $i$ or $k$ form a link to an agent $r$ outside the \
group.\\nWithout group influence, these probabilities would only depend on \
$x_{i}$, $x_{k}$ and $x_{r}$.\\nWith group influence, \
$x_{i}^{\\\\mathrm{eff}}$ and $x_{k}^{\\\\mathrm{eff}}$ become more similar, \
so the likelihood that the link is formed inside the group increases.\\nThis \
process will lead to the \\\\emph{densification} of the group to improve \
triadic closure. \\n\\n\\n\\n\\\\subsection{Network \
formation}\\n\\\\label{sec:basic-mechanisms}\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{Percolation \
threshold. \\\\ }\\n\\nWe now change the perspective from the group level to \
the system level, which can be described as a large social network.\\nThe \
$a_{ij}$ defined in Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:19} are the entries of a symmetric \
adjacency matrix $\\\\mathcal{A}$ of size $N\\\\times N$, which captures the \
topology of the network.\\n\\nA group is a \\\\emph{disconnected component} \
of this network.\\nInitially, social groups may form spontaneously in a \
random  manner.\\nTherefore in an early stage of the network evolution we may \
expect a larger number of disconnected components. \\nThe number of these \
disconnected components, their sizes and their  densities can grow and shrink \
over time. \\nA phase transition is characterized by the emergence of a \
\\\\emph{giant connected component}, i.e. one group of macroscopic size that \
includes most of the agents.\\n\\nTo better understand the conditions under \
which we can expect the formation of a giant connected component in the \
network, let us turn to one of the simplest network models, known as the \
$G(N,M)$ model.\\nIt requires to fix the number of agents, $N$, and the total \
number of links, $M$.\\nInitially there is no network.\\nIt is formed \
successively by randomly choosing, out of $N$ agents, two agents $i$ and $j$ \
and connect them by a link. \\nEach established link diminishes the number of \
available links by one.\\nThe process is repeated until all $M$ links are \
spent.\\nThe result is a \\\\emph{random network} with characteristic \
properties that can be described analytically \
\\\\citep{Bollob_s_2001}.\\n\\nThe $G(N,p)$ model looses the assumption of a \
fixed $M$ \\\\citep{gilbert_1959}.\\nInstead, two randomly chosen agents are \
connected with a certain probability $p$, which is determined such that it \
matches the \\\\emph{expected degree} $\\\\mean{d}$, i.e. the expected number \
of links.\\nIf $N^{2}/2$ is the maximum number of links between $N$ agents \
and $M$ the total number of links, we then find for \
$p$:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:18}\\n  \
p=\\\\frac{2\\\\mean{M}}{N^{2}}\\\\;; \\\\quad \
\\\\mean{d}=\\\\frac{2\\\\mean{M}}{N}=pN\\n\\\\end{align}\\n\\nThe question \
whether we will observe a giant connected component from this simple \
procedure depends on the total number of links, $M$, which has to have a \
critical value, known as the percolation threshold.\\nBecause we do not \
assume any underlying geometry, such as lattices, the percolation threshold \
results from the condition $Np=1$ \
\\\\citep{molloy_1995,schweitzer_2021}.\\nUsing the relations from \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:18}, it follows directly that a percolating group can be \
expected if $M^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}=N/2$.\\n\\nThis allows us to define an \
initial supersaturation for \\\\emph{links} \
$\\\\mu=M/M^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$.\\nTo expect a phase transition via \
percolation, we have to assume that\\n \
$\\\\mu=\\\\mu^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}=1$.\\nThis result holds for infinite systems.\
\\nFor finite systems, this value has to be larger as we will show in \
Sect.~\\\\ref{sec:percolation}.\\nNevertheless, $\\\\mu^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}=1$ \
is a good reference value to distinguish whether a system has the ability of \
a phase transition.\\nWe could compare it to the supersaturation introduced \
in Sect.~\\\\ref{sec:mech-phase-trans}, \
$y=\\\\rho/\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{eq}}(T)$, which has the same interpretation, \
just in a thermodynamic context. \\n\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{Restricted link \
formation. \\\\ }\\n\\nThe above discussion holds as long as agents are not \
restricted in their link formation.\\nIn our model, however, we have \
introduced a threshold $\\\\epsilon$, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:19}, which determines \
whether two agents $i$ and $j$ can interact, i.e. can potentially form a \
link. \\nSo far $\\\\epsilon$ was large enough to allow link formation \
between \\\\emph{all} agents. \\nNow we consider that not all agents can \
create links to all other agents, i.e. $\\\\epsilon\\\\ll 1$.\\n\\nTo still \
ensure a phase transition, we have to calculate the critical  value of \
$\\\\epsilon$ needed to allow for a sufficient number of links.\\nFor this we \
follow an earlier approach \\\\citep{schweitzer_2021} that links percolation \
in random networks with so-called \\\\emph{threshold networks}, i.e. networks \
where the link formation is restricted by the tolerance threshold \
$\\\\epsilon$.\\n\\nWe remind that the $x_{i}$ are uniformly distributed in \
the interval $[0,1]$ and that for the formation of links $\\\\Delta x_{ij}=\\\
\\abs{x_{j}-x_{i}}$ matters.\\nThe distribution function $P(\\\\Delta {x})$ \
of the absolute difference between two uniform variables is given by the \
\\\\emph{triangular distribution}.\\nThe cumulative probability $F(\\\\Delta \
{x} \\\\leq \\\\epsilon)$ to find a value $\\\\Delta {x} \\\\leq \\\\epsilon$ \
follows likewise:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:5}\\n  P(\\\\Delta {x}) \
=&\\\\ 2 -2 \\\\left(\\\\Delta {x} \\\\right) \\\\;; \\\\quad 0\\\\leq \
\\\\Delta {x} < 1 \\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\nF(\\\\Delta {x} \\\\leq \
\\\\epsilon) = &\\\\ 2 \\\\epsilon - \\\\epsilon^{2}\\n\\\\end{align}\\n$F(\\\
\\epsilon)$ is the probability that for a randomly chosen pair of agents a \
link is \\\\emph{possible}.\\nThis has to be multiplied by the independent \
probability that this pair of agents is also chosen to form a link, which is \
given by $p$.\\nHence, the condition for percolation $Np=1$ has to be \
corrected by the factor $F(\\\\epsilon)$ that a link formation could not be \
possible if opinions deviate.\\nWith this consideration and \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:18} we find:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:34}\\nNp\\\
\\,F(\\\\epsilon)=\\\\frac{2\\\\mean{M}}{N}\\\\left[2\\\\epsilon \
-\\\\epsilon^{2}\\\\right]  \\n\\\\end{align}\\nFrom this quadratic equation \
for $\\\\epsilon$ \\nonly the lower value makes sense in our model because  $\
\\\\epsilon$ has to have values between 0 and 1.\\nWith $\\\\mu=2M/N$, it \
solves into:\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n  \\\\label{eq:3}\\n  \
\\\\epsilon^{\\\\mathrm{cr}} = 1 - \\\\sqrt{1 - \
\\\\frac{1}{\\\\mu}}\\n\\\\end{equation}\\n$\\\\epsilon^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$ \
allows us to choose the tolerance for deviating opinions such that still a \
giant connected component can emerge, dependent on the link supersaturation $\
\\\\mu$.  \\nIf $\\\\mu=\\\\mu^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}=1$, i.e. we just have the \
conditions for the onset of percolation in an infinite system, \
$\\\\epsilon=1$, i.e. we cannot allow for restrictions in the \
interactions.\\nThe higher the link supersaturation, the lower $\\\\epsilon$ \
can be to still observe a giant connected component.\\n\\nFor $\\\\epsilon< \
\\\\epsilon^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$, we can only expect the formation of \
disconnected components.\\nThis is similar to the bounded confidence model, \
where  the value of $\\\\epsilon$ determines the final number of groups that \
can each reach consensus.\\nFor this number, a rough estimate \
$1/(2\\\\epsilon)$ was found. \\nThat means one group, i.e. a giant connected \
component, can be only obtained for $\\\\epsilon\\\\geq \
0.5$.\\n$\\\\epsilon\\\\leq 0.25$ would already result in two groups, i.e. \
two disconnected components \\\\citep{deffuant_2000,lorenz_2007}.\\n\\nIn our \
case, for $\\\\mu=3$, for instance, we find a giant connected component for $\
\\\\epsilon\\\\geq 0.2$.\\nThis comparably low value becomes clear, because \
the giant connected component is a ``spanning cluster'', i.e. it can be a \
very sparse network, as long as it spans the whole system.\\nIn \
Sect.~\\\\ref{sec:clust-form-netw}, \\nwe will illustrate how such giant \
connected components look like.\\n\\nWe note that Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:3} is \
derived under the assumption of uniformly distributed $x_{i}$.\\nIt does not \
consider a change of this distribution at the same time scale as the \
formation of the giant connected component.\\nHence, in order to apply \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:3} we have to assume that $\\\\gamma$ in \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:mean} is considerably small.\\nIn fact, in \
Figure~\\\\ref{fig:perc} we later use $\\\\gamma$=0.1, and we further show in \
Figure~\\\\ref{fig:time} that in the percolation scenario the formation of \
the largest group occurs very fast.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\"\>"}, {4,
  "4"->"\<\"{Simulating group formation in \
networks}\\n\\\\label{sec:clust-form-netw}\\n\\n\\\\subsection{Transition \
rates}\\n\\\\label{sec:transition-rates}\\n\\nIn the following we lay out a \
stochastic dynamics for the formation of groups.\\nAs the discussion in \
Sect.~\\\\ref{sec:mech-phase-trans} made clear, we need to consider \
fluctuations in order to describe the spontaneous formation of groups and \
their growth up to a critical size.\\nThe dynamics of groups with a \
supercritical size, on the other hand, can be described by a deterministic \
dynamics, which will be developed in \
Section~\\\\ref{sec:analyt-invest}.\\n\\nThe fundamental dynamics of \
stochastic processes is described by a master equation.\\nThis can be defined \
at different levels.\\nThe most explicit perspective starts from the \
adjacency matrix that contains all information about links between \
agents.\\nOur focus is \\\\emph{not} on the description of the full network, \
but on the dynamics of individual groups.\\nThis leaves us still with the \
choice between the group level or the full group distribution, \
$\\\\pmb{N}=[N_{1,0},N_{2,1},N_{3,2},N_{3,3},...,N_{N,M}]$.\\nThe elements \
$N_{n,m}$ count the number of groups of size $n$ with $m$ links.\\nThis \
approach is summarized in appendix B.\\nIn the following, we focus on the \
group level because we do not depend on the full description of the dynamics \
of the state space.\\nIn fact, all our equations can be derived from the \
simpler perspective.\\nHence, \\nin our case we start from \
$p[g_{k}(n_{k},m_{k},t)]$, the probability to find group $k$ with $n_{k}$ \
agents and $m_{k}$ links at time $t$.\\n\\n\\n\\nThe specification of the \
dynamics requires to define the respective \\\\emph{transition rates} for the \
change of these probabilities.\\nThis can be done in different ways.\\nLet us \
first discuss the perspective of \\\\emph{agents}.\\nTo determine the \
formation of a link $(i,j)$ between two randomly chosen agents $i$ and $j$, \
we have to consider the probabilities (i) that agents $i$ and $j$ are chosen, \
which is $1/N$ for each in a random sampling, and (ii) that they are able to \
establish  a link which depends on the value of $z_{ij}$, \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:33}, and (iii) that links are still available. \\n\\n$M$ is \
the initial number of links which is limited because we distinguish between \
interactions and links.\\nThe latter are only formed under certain conditions \
which are socially motivated by the opinion dynamics already \
discussed.\\nSocial networks are sparse, therefore, $M$ has to be smaller to \
avoid fully connected networks.\\nSimilar to the $G(N,M)$ model, the fixed \
$M$ also determines the probability of link formation.\\n\\n\\nIf links were \
unrestricted, the system could evolve to maximal density always.\\nIn real \
social systems, interactions can be abundant, but social relations, i.e. \
links, are usually limited, e.g. by mental capacities.  We remind on the \
Dunbar number.\\nThe restriction is further given by the limited size of the \
groups.\\nWe are considering finite systems with $N$ agents, hence the \
maximum number of relations would be $M=N(N-1)/2$.\\nIf we would go with this \
number, the model would end up with fully connected \
networks.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nBut at time $t$ some groups have already been \
formed.\\nTherefore we have\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:1}\\n  \
M=m_{0}(t) +\\\\sum_{k=1}^{K}m_{k}(t)\\\\;;\\\\quad\\n   N=n_{0}(t) \
+\\\\sum_{k=1}^{K}n_{k}(t)\\n\\\\end{align}\\n\\n$m_{0}(t)$ denotes the \
number of free links and $n_{0}(t)$ the number of free agents that do not \
belong to any group at time $t$.\\nWith these assumptions the transition rate \
for link formation reads:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:20}\\n  \
w\\\\left[(i,j)|i,j\\\\right]\\\\propto \\\\frac{2 \
m_{0}}{N^{2}}\\\\Theta[z_{ij}(t)] \\n\\\\end{align}\\nIn the limit of \
$\\\\epsilon\\\\to 1$, $\\\\Theta[z]=1$.\\nWe note that this rate holds no \
matter whether agents are already part of a group or not. \\n\\nWe can \
describe the same process from the \\\\emph{group} perspective.\\nEach group \
is characterized by two values $n_{k}$, $m_{k}$.\\nWhen we randomly choose \
two agents, the probability that one of the agents is in group $k$ and the \
other one in group $l$ is proportional to $n_{k}$ and $n_{l}$.\\nWith these \
consideration  the transition rate for a growing group reads in \
general:\\n\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:21}\\n  \
w\\\\left[(n_{k}+n_{l},m_{k}+m_{l}+1)|(n_{k},m_{k}), (n_{l},m_{l})\\\\right] \
= w^{+}\\\\left[g_{k},g_{l}\\\\right] \\\\propto \\\\frac{2 m_{0}}{N^{2}}\\n  \
\\\\sum_{i\\\\in g_{k}}\\\\sum_{j\\\\in g_{l}}\\\\Theta[z_{ij}(t)] \
\\n\\\\end{align}\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nIn the limit of $\\\\epsilon\\\\to \
1$, $\\\\Theta[z]=1$ the summation term becomes $n_{k}n_{l}$.\\nThis rate \
describes the process of \\\\emph{coagulation}.\\nAdding one link between two \
groups $g_{k}$, $g_{l}$ leads them to merge, i.e. to form one connected group \
of size $(n_{k}+n_{l},m_{k}+m_{l}+1)$.\\nThis can be seen as a jump in the \
$(n,m)$ space, as illustrated in Figure~\\\\ref{fig:symbolic \
representation}(a).\\n\\nIf an agent $i$ has not joined a group yet, it \
defines its own group, i.e. then $n_{i}$ = 1 and $m_{i}$ = 0 and \
$g_{i}(n_{i},m_{i})=g_{1}(1,0)$.\\nIn this case, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:21} \
captures the   process of  \\\\emph{incremental growth} and reads \
specifically:\\n\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:21-inc}\\n  \
w\\\\left[(n_{k}+1,m_{k}+1)|(n_{k},m_{k}), (1,0)\\\\right] = \
w^{+}\\\\left[g_{k},g_{1}\\\\right] \\\\propto \\\\frac{2 m_{0}}{N^{2}}\\n  \
\\\\sum_{i\\\\in g_{k}}\\\\sum_{j\\\\in g_{1}}\\\\Theta[z_{ij}(t)] \
\\n\\\\end{align}\\n\\nAdding one link between a group $g_{k}(n_{k},m_{k})$ \
and an isolated agent, $g_{1}(1,0)$ grows both $n_{k}$ and $m_{k}$ by 1, \
incrementally, as shown in Figure~\\\\ref{fig:symbolic representation}(b).\\n\
\\n\\n\\\\begin{figure}[htbp] \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.99\\\\linewidth]{figures/figure1-standalone.pdf}\\\
n \\\\caption{Illustration of the three growth mechanisms for groups: (a) \
coagulation, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:21}, (b) incremental growth, \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:21-inc}, (c) densification, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:14}.}\\n  \
\\\\label{fig:symbolic representation}\\n      \
\\\\end{figure}\\n\\nAdditionally, there can be also a  \
\\\\emph{densification} of the group, illustrated in \
Figure~\\\\ref{fig:symbolic representation}(c). \\nIf the two agents already \
belong to the same group, adding one link does not change the number of \
agents, but only the number of links and this way the density.\\nThe new \
group is then given by $g_{k}(n_{k},m_{k}+1)$.\\nIn general, the transition \
rate for densification reads:\\n\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n  \
\\\\label{eq:100}\\n  w\\\\left[(n_{k},m_{k}+1)|(n_{k},m_{k})\\\\right]\\n  \
\\\\propto  \\\\frac{2 m_{0}}{N^{2}}\\\\sum_{\\\\substack{i,j \\\\in n_{k} \\\
\\\\\\ \\\\{i,j\\\\}\\\\not\\\\in m_{k}}}\\\\delta_{ij} \
\\\\Theta[z_{ij}]\\n\\\\end{equation}\\n\\n\\nThe sum over the Kronecker \
$\\\\delta_{ij}$ counts only \\nagents $i$ and $j$ that both belong to the \
group of $n$ agents, but do not have a common link  yet.\\nIf we assume \
$\\\\epsilon=1$, i.e. $\\\\Theta[z_{ij}]=1$, \\nthe probability $p_{ij}$ that \
such a link already exists can be calculated from the configuration model as:\
\\n\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n  \\\\label{eq:11}\\n  p_{ij} = \
\\\\frac{d_{i}d_{j}}{2{m_{k}}}\\n\\\\end{equation}\\n\\n\\n$d_{i}$ and \
$d_{j}$ are the degrees of agents $i$ and $j$, hence the product considers \
all possibilities to connect them.\\nThis has to be normalized by $m_{k}$, \
the existing number of links in the group $k$.\\n\\nWe further know that in a \
group of density $\\\\rho_{k}$ each agent has on average \
$\\\\mean{d}_{k}=2m_{k}/n_{k}=\\\\rho_{k}$ links.\\nWe use this to replace \
$d_{i}$ and $d_{j}$ in Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:11}.\\nHence, the sum in \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:100} is expressed by the probability that agents $i$ and \
$j$ are chosen to form a link, which is proportional to $n_{k}^{2}$, \
multiplied by the independent  probability that they do not already have one:\
\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n  \\\\label{eq:120}\\n  p_{ij} \\\\equiv p_{k} = \
\\\\frac{\\\\rho_{k}^{2}}{2m_{k}} = \\\\frac{2m_{k}}{n_{k}^{2}} \
\\\\;;\\\\quad\\n  \\\\sum_{\\\\substack{i,j \\\\in n_{k} \\\\\\\\ \\\\{i,j\\\
\\}\\\\not\\\\in m_{k}}}\\\\delta_{i,j} =\\n  \
n_{k}^{2}\\\\left[1-\\\\frac{2m_{k}}{n_{k}^{2}} \
\\\\right]\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nThis  allows to rewrite, for \
$\\\\epsilon=1$, the transition rate for \\\\emph{densification}, \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:100} as:\\n\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:14}\\n  w\\\
\\left[(n_{k},m_{k}+1)|(n_{k},m_{k})\\\\right] =\\n  w^{+}\\\\left[ \
g_{k},m_{0}\\\\right] &\\\\propto  \\\\frac{2 m_{0}}{N^{2}}n_{k}\\n  \
\\\\left[n_{k}-\\\\rho_{k} \\\\right]\\n\\\\end{align}\\n\\n\\nThe difference \
$\\\\omega_{k}=n_{k}-\\\\rho_{k}$ tells how many links could be potentially \
added to group $k$.\\nHence, this transition rate decreases with higher \
density.\\nFurthermore, when the group is fully connected, we have $m_{k} = \
{n_{k}(n_{k}-1)}/{2}$. This means that in the limit of large systems \
($n_{k}(n_{k}-1)\\\\rightarrow n_{k}^{2}$) no new link can be formed, which \
is reasonable.\\nFor finite systems this holds only approximately.\\n\\nWith \
this, we have considered three different processes for network \
\\\\emph{growth} via the formation of groups. \\nBut we also need to specify \
how groups can dissolve \\\\citep{Carro2014}.\\nFor this, we have already \
proposed in Sect.~\\\\ref{sec:mech-group-form} that agents leave a group if \
they experience an negative utility, i.e. if costs exceed \
benefits.\\nAccording to Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:24}, this implies that for a group \
$g_{k}$, it holds that $\\\\rho_{k}<\\\\hat{\\\\rho}$.\\nWe propose that \
agents leave a group \\\\emph{spontaneously} at the following \
rate:\\n\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:400}\\nw\\\\left[(n_{k}-1,m_{k}-\
\\\\delta m_{k}) | (n_{k},m_{k})\\\\right] = \
w^{-}\\\\left[g_{k},\\\\rho_{k}\\\\right] \\\\propto \\\\frac{n_{k}}{N}  \
\\\\exp{\\\\left\\\\{\\\\beta\\\\frac{\\\\hat{\\\\rho}}{\\\\rho_{k}}\\\\right\
\\\\}}\\n\\\\end{align}\\n\\n$n_{k}/N$ is again the probability that an agent \
from group $g_{k}$ is randomly chosen to leave.\\nThe exponential term \
${\\\\hat{\\\\rho}/\\\\rho_{k}} = (c n_{k})/(b m_{k})$\\ncan be seen as some \
sort of inverse fitness of a group.\\nIf it is larger than one, which means \
costs are larger than benefits, the probability that the group dissolves \
spontaneously increase exponentially.\\n$\\\\beta$ is the inverse \
temperature, $1/T$, and defines the level of randomness. \\nSmaller \
$\\\\beta$ make differences between benefits and costs more important, larger \
$\\\\beta$ smooth out this influence.\\n\\nIf an agent leaves a group, all \
its links in the group are removed and become available as free \
links.\\nThus, the group size $n_{k}$ is deminished by 1.\\nBut how many \
links will be removed?\\nWe know that in a group of density $\\\\rho_{k}$ \
each agent has on average $\\\\mean{d}_{k}=\\\\rho_{k}$ links.\\nThat means, \
the expected change is $\\\\delta m_{k}=\\\\rho_{k}$ in \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:400}.\\nWe note that this transition rate in principle also \
describes the \\\\emph{fragmentation} of a group of size $n_{k}$ into pieces.\
\\nIn this case just the arguments of $w^{-}$ have to be changed, the \
probability for a spontaneous leave remains the same.\\n\\nWe summarize, that \
the fastest way for a network to \\\\emph{grow} is by coagulation. \\nOnce \
some groups have formed, they can merge into much larger groups.\\nThis helps \
to speed up a possible phase transition.\\nIf coagulation is excluded, i.e. \
$n_{l}\\\\equiv 1$, the phase transition would occur much slower because \
incremental growth is a rather slow process.\\nSo we could see it as the  \
\\\\emph{worst case} scenario.\\nDensification, on the other hand, does not \
lead to growth,\\nbut \\\\emph{stabilizes} existing groups because it \
increases the group utility.\\nBut even if the density is increased, as long \
as $\\\\rho_{k}<\\\\hat{\\\\rho}$, staying in such a group would not be \
beneficial.\\nHence, groups with a smaller density are likely to shrink and \
to disappear, no matter what their size $n_{k}$ \
is.\\n\\n\\n\\\\subsection{Results of agent-based computer \
simulations}\\n\\\\label{sec:results-agent-based}\\n\\nUsing the above \
defined transition rates, we can write down $k$ master equations \
\\\\citep{Roepke_2013} for the growth and dissolution of groups.\\nFor the \
probability $p[g_{k}(n_{k},m_{k},t)]$ the stochastic dynamics reads  in \
general:\\n\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:8}\\n  \\\\frac{\\\\Delta \
p[g_{k}(n_{k},m_{k},t)]}{\\\\Delta t}=& \\\\sum\\\\nolimits_{g_{l}} \
w^{+}\\\\left[g_{k},g_{l}\\\\right]\\\\;  p[g_{k}(n_{k}-n_{l},m_{k}-m_{l},t) \
\\\\cap g_{l}(n_{l},m_{l},t)]  \\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n   & - \
w^{+}\\\\left[g_{k},g_{l}\\\\right]\\\\;  p[g_{k}(n_{k},m_{k},t) \\\\cap \
g_{l}(n_{l},m_{l},t)]  \\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n                               \
         &  + w^{+}\\\\left[g_{k},m_{0}\\\\right] \\\\; \
p[g_{k}(n_{k},m_{k}-1,t)]  - w^{+}\\\\left[g_{k},m_{0}\\\\right]\\\\;  \
p[g_{k}(n_{k},m_{k},t)] \\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n                              \
                    &    + w^{-}\\\\left[ g_{k},\\\\rho_{k}\\\\right] \\\\; \
p[g_{k}(n_{k} + n_{l},m_{k} + m_{l},t) \\\\cap g_{l}(n_{l},m_{l}, t+\\\\Delta \
t)] \\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n                                                 \
&   -  w^{-}\\\\left[ g_{k},\\\\rho_{k}\\\\right] \\\\; \
p[g_{k}(n_{k},m_{k},t)]\\n\\\\end{align}\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nNote that the master \
equations for the $k$ different groups are coupled via the transition rates \
that have to satisfy the boundary condition, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:1}.\\nTo \
simulate the dynamics we use the Gillespie algorithm.\\nThat means, for each \
time step we calculate all possible transition rates.\\nTheir relative weight \
is used to randomly select one of the possible transitions.\\nIn the \
following we present simulation results for the three different scenarios for \
phase transitions described in Sect.~\\\\ref{sec:mech-phase-trans}.\\nWe \
decided to keep these physical labels for the scenarios even for the dynamics \
of social groups, simply to allow for comparison. \
\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{Percolation. \\\\ }\\n\\\\label{sec:percolation}\\n\\nThe \
simplest scenario of group formation assumes that agents connect randomly, \
this way forming a \\\\textrm{random network}.\\nWe do \\\\emph{not} consider \
any costs or benefits for groups, further \\\\emph{no} spontaneous \
dissolution is allowed.\\nAlso, we ignore for the moment any dynamics or \
influence of the $x_{i}$ values in the formation of groups ($\\\\gamma$ = 0) \
and link formation between all agents is allowed ($\\\\epsilon$ = 1).  \
\\nHence, the whole dynamics is described by the transition rate for growth, \
$w^{+}\\\\left[g_{k},g_{l}\\\\right]$, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:21}. \\nIn an early \
stage we find a larger number of disconnected components, as shown in Figure~\
\\\\ref{fig:perc}(a),\\nThese disconnected components can only \\\\emph{grow} \
over time as long as there are free links available. \\nEventually, they \
merge to form a giant connected component, shown in \
Figure~\\\\ref{fig:perc}(c). \\n\\n\\\\begin{figure}[htbp] \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.28\\\\textwidth]{figures/Percolation/\
NonRestricted/network_20.png}\\n  (a) \\\\hfill\\n   \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.28\\\\textwidth]{figures/Percolation/\
NonRestricted/network_50.png}\\n  (b) \\\\hfill\\n   \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.28\\\\textwidth]{figures/Percolation/\
NonRestricted/network_100.png}\\n  (c)\\n  \\\\caption{Phase transition via \
percolation. Different time steps: (a) $t$ =  0.14, (b) $t$ = 0.55, (c) $t$ = \
1.42. Parameters: $\\\\mu/2$ = 1.6, $c$ = 0, $\\\\epsilon$ = 1, $\\\\gamma$ = \
0. The color scheme indicates the $x_{i}$ values of the agents, which do not \
change.}\\n  \
\\\\label{fig:perc}\\n\\\\end{figure}\\n\\n\\n\\\\begin{figure}[htbp]  \
\\\\centering\\n  \\\\includegraphics[width=0.45\\\\textwidth, trim={0 0 0 \
2.5cm},clip]{figures/percol_Dens_lcc2.pdf}(a)\\n    \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.45\\\\textwidth, trim={0 0 0 \
2.5cm},clip]{figures/restrictedPercolEps01.pdf}(b)\\n    \\\\caption{Fraction \
of the largest connected group $\\\\nu=n^{\\\\mathrm{LCC}}/N$ for different $\
\\\\mu=M/M^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$.\\n      (a)       Unconstrained interactions ($\
\\\\epsilon$ = 1), (b) \\n      constrained interactions $\\\\epsilon$ = 0.1.\
\\n      The dashed lines show the theoretical predictions for the\\n      \
percolation threshold: (a) $\\\\mu/2$ = 0.5, (b) $\\\\mu/2$ = 2.63, \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:3}.\\n    }\\n  \
\\\\label{fig:thresh-pc}\\n\\\\end{figure}\\n\\nFigure~\\\\ref{fig:thresh-pc} \
shows the fraction of the largest connected component, \
$\\\\nu=n^{\\\\mathrm{LCC}}/N$, in the final network dependent on the initial \
condition $\\\\mu=M/M^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$, i.e. the total number of available \
links relative to the critical number of links that are needed for \
percolation.\\nAccording to the discussion in \
Sect.~\\\\ref{sec:basic-mechanisms}, we expect the percolation threshold to \
be $\\\\mu^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$ = 1, which only holds for very large systems and \
without any restrictions on the link formation, i.e. $\\\\epsilon\\\\to 1$. \
\\nAs Figure~\\\\ref{fig:thresh-pc}(a) shows, for finite systems, $N=100$, \
$\\\\mu^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$ only indicates the \\\\emph{onset} of forming a \
percolating group.\\nTo observe percolation with a fraction of the LCC close \
to one, we   need a five times larger value, as \
Figure~\\\\ref{fig:thresh-pc}(a) shows. \\n\\nWhen restricted interactions \
between agents are considered, i.e. $\\\\epsilon<1$, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:3} \
defines the conditions for observing a giant connected component.\\nFor \
$\\\\epsilon$ = 0.1, we find $\\\\mu$ = 2.63.\\nThe computer simulations \
shown in Figure~\\\\ref{fig:thresh-pc}(b) give a better match with the \
prediction, also because in Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:3} the finite number of links \
is explicitly considered.\\n\\nWe conclude that the percolation scenario is \
observed for  $\\\\mu \\\\simeq 5$, $c=0$.\\nStill, because the link \
supersaturation $\\\\mu$ is not large, we will only obtain a \\\\emph{a \
sparse percolating group}.\\nThe initial density of free links is not large \
enough for a compact phase, as we show below. \\n\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{Spinodal \
decomposition. \\\\ }\\n\\\\label{sec:spin-decomp}\\nAs explained in Sect.~\\\
\\ref{sec:mech-phase-trans} this scenario is observed if the initial system \
is already in an unstable state.\\nFurther, the surface tension does not give \
an important effect.\\nIn our model of group formation, this is realized by  \
a very large link supersaturation $\\\\mu\\\\gg 1$ and by a negligible cost \
$c\\\\ll 1$.\\nThen the initial  instability is sufficient for the \
spontaneous emergence of a new phase, which is rather compact. \\nThis is \
illustrated in Figure~\\\\ref{fig:spin-sim}. \\n\\\\begin{figure}[htbp] \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.28\\\\textwidth]{figures/Spinodal_decomposition/\
network_100.png}\\n  (a) \\\\hfill\\n   \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.28\\\\textwidth]{figures/Spinodal_decomposition/\
network_200.png}\\n  (b) \\\\hfill\\n   \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.28\\\\textwidth]{figures/Spinodal_decomposition/\
network_800.png}\\n  (c)\\n  \\\\caption{Phase transition via spinodal \
decomposition. Different time steps: (a) $t$ = 0.08, (b) $t$ = 0.13, (c) $t$ \
= 0.56. Parameters: $\\\\mu/2$ = 19, $c$ = 0.5, $N$ = 100, $\\\\epsilon$ = 1, \
$\\\\gamma$ = 0.1. The color scheme indicates the $x_{i}$ values of the \
agents.}\\n  \
\\\\label{fig:spin-sim}\\n\\\\end{figure}\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{\
Nucleation. \\\\ }\\n\\\\label{sec:nucleation}\\n\\nTo observe \
\\\\emph{nucleation}, the initial system has to be in a clear metastable \
state.\\nIn our model of group formation this is realized by a  moderate link \
supersaturation $\\\\mu \\\\gg 1$ and a non negligible cost $c>0$.\\nThen, \
the new phase can only emerge if initial fluctuations generate groups larger \
than a critical size, discussed in detail in \
Sect.~\\\\ref{sec:crit-clust-size}. \\nThe phase transition occurs via the \
formation of a number of groups  in an early stage, shown in \
Figure~\\\\ref{fig:nucleation}(a).\\nThese group later either coalesce or \
they form a macroscopic phase via the redistribution of links from groups \
with subcritical to groups with supercritical sizes.\\nThis process is known \
as Ostwald ripening and  will be further discussed in \
Section~\\\\ref{sec:ostwald-ripening}.\\n\\n\\\\begin{figure}[htbp] \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.28\\\\textwidth]{figures/lowIngroup_highEpsilon/\
network_2000.png}\\n  (a) \\\\hfill\\n   \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.28\\\\textwidth]{figures/lowIngroup_highEpsilon/\
network_5200.png}\\n  (b) \\\\hfill\\n   \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.28\\\\textwidth]{figures/lowIngroup_highEpsilon/\
network_6000.png}\\n  (c)\\n \\\\caption{Phase transition via nucleation. \
Different time steps: (a) $t$ = 0.7, (b) $t$ = 2.07, (c) $t$ = 2.67. \
Parameters: $\\\\mu/2$ = 16, $\\\\hat \\\\rho$ = 15.6, $N$ = 100, \
$\\\\epsilon$ = 1, $\\\\gamma$ = 0.5, $\\\\beta$ = 0.4. The color scheme \
indicates the $x_{i}$ values of the agents.}\\n  \\\\label{fig:nucleation}\\n\
\\\\end{figure}\\n\\nA dynamics, where initially small groups are formed and \
later merge or dissolve to give way for a giant connected component, is found \
in the evolution of collaboration networks.\\nIn \\\\citep{Tomasello2017}, \
\\ngroups represent R\\\\&D alliances in one or multiple industrial sectors \
over 20 years, as reconstructed from the SDC Platinum database. \\nA \
group-size distribution akin to our intermediate regime ($t$ = $1.2$) in \
Figure~\\\\ref{fig:group-dist}(a) can be found in these R\\\\&D networks \
\\\\citep{tomasello_2014}.\\n\\nA similar dynamics is also observed in the \
evolution of scientific collaboration networks reconstructed from the Social \
Work Research Database \\\\citep{eckl_2018}.\\nFrom initially disconnected \
groups, this system generates over time one prominent component encompassing \
up to 20\\\\% of the nodes in later stages.\\nMany other disconnected \
component remain in the system to coexist with the giant \
component.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{Restricted interactions. \\\\  \
}\\n\\\\label{sec:restr-inter}\\n\\\\label{sec:constant-x_i}\\n\\nAs the two \
examples of Figures~\\\\ref{fig:spin-sim},~\\\\ref{fig:nucleation} show, the \
emerging giant connected components become rather homogeneous with respect to \
the opinions $x_{i}$.\\nThis is due to the fact that all agents are allowed \
to form links ($\\\\epsilon$ = 1) and the opinion dynamics, \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:mean}, ensures convergence of opinions ($\\\\gamma$ = 0.1).\
\\nIf we restrict the interaction between agents by choosing \
$\\\\epsilon<\\\\epsilon^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:3}, we observe \
the formation of \\\\emph{isolated} groups instead of a giant connected \
component.\\nThese groups differ considerably with respect to their average $\
\\\\mean{x^{k}}$, but show rather homogeneous values of $x_{i}$ inside each \
group, as Figure~\\\\ref{fig:thres-t} shows.\\n\\n\\n\\\\begin{figure}[htbp] \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.28\\\\textwidth]{figures/Percolation/Restricted/\
network_200.png}\\n  (a) \\\\hfill\\n   \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.28\\\\textwidth]{figures/Percolation/Restricted/\
network_500.png}\\n  (b) \\\\hfill\\n   \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.28\\\\textwidth]{figures/Percolation/Restricted/\
network_1000.png}\\n  (c)\\n  \\\\caption{Phase transition with restricted \
interactions. Different time steps:  (a) $t$ = 0.25, (b) $t$ = 0.68, (c) $t$ \
= 2.15. Parameters: $\\\\mu/2$ = 4, $c$ = 0, $N$ = 100, $\\\\epsilon$ = \
0.125, $\\\\gamma$ = 0.1. The color scheme indicates the $x_{i}$ values of \
the agents. }\\n  \\\\label{fig:thres-t}\\n\\\\end{figure}\\n\\nAgain, \
collaboration networks show a dynamics where multiple disconnected components \
with non-negligible sizes survive. \\nWe reach sparse disconnected components \
 when the saturation $\\\\mu$ is low.  \\nEvidence for the coexistence of \
sparse disconnected groups is found  for instance in the collaboration \
network of Italian sociologists \
\\\\citep{akbaritabar_2020}.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\\\subsection{Parameter space} \
\\\\label{sec:Phase Diagrams}\\n\\nWe can systematize the outcome of our \
agent-based computer simulations with respect to the free parameters of our \
group formation model.\\nThese can be distinguished in three groups (see \
appendix A for table \
representation):\\n\\\\begin{enumerate}[itemsep=0em]\\n\\\\item \
\\\\emph{network formation}: number of agents $N$, link density \
$\\\\mu=2M/N$, level of randomness $\\\\beta$,\\n\\\\item \\\\emph{group \
formation}: cost $c$, benefit $b$, or $\\\\hat{\\\\rho}=c/b$,\\n\\\\item \
\\\\emph{opinion formation}: approach rate $\\\\gamma$, tolerance threshold $\
\\\\epsilon$, group influence $\\\\alpha$. \\n\\\\end{enumerate}\\nComparing \
these parameters with their thermodynamic counterparts from \
Sect.~\\\\ref{sec:mech-phase-trans}, we see that both $N$ and $\\\\mu$ relate \
to the initial supersaturation while the cost $c$, or $\\\\hat \\\\rho$, \
relates to the surface tension, because it determines the critical group \
size.\\nThe parameters of the opinion dynamics do not play a role in the \
thermodynamic model, because for molecules no constrains exist to form \
droplets.\\n\\n\\\\begin{figure}[htbp]  \\n  \\\\includegraphics[width=0.99\\\
\\linewidth]{figures/figure7-standalone.pdf}\\n\\\\caption{\\n  $(\\\\mu/2,\\\
\\hat{\\\\rho})$ parameter space for the size of the largest connected \
component $n^{\\\\mathrm{LCC}}$. $N$ = 100, different parameters for opinion \
dynamics: (left) $\\\\epsilon$ = 0.16, $\\\\alpha$ =\\n0.9, $\\\\gamma$ = \
0.1, $\\\\beta$ = 0.4, (right) $\\\\epsilon$ = 1, $\\\\alpha$ = 0.1, \
$\\\\gamma$ = 0.5, $\\\\beta$ = 0.4.\\n  }\\n  \\\\label{fig: \
phaseDiagHighTension}\\n\\\\end{figure}\\n\\nIn Figure~\\\\ref{fig: \
phaseDiagHighTension} we present two phase diagrams of the $(\\\\mu/2,\\\\hat\
\\\\rho)$ parameter space,  with different parameters for opinion formation \
(left) and (right).\\nThese diagrams have no sharp phase boundaries, which \
could be only expected in the thermodynamic limit but not in finite \
systems.\\nNevertheless, the phase diagrams succinctly illustrate the \
influence of the parameters on the group structure.\\n\\nOur measure for the \
existence of a phase transition is $n^{\\\\mathrm{LCC}}$, the size of the \
largest connected component (LCC), measured after 50.000 time steps, i.e. in \
a quasi stationary equilibrium.\\nWith $N$ = 100, $n^{\\\\mathrm{LCC}}$ can \
reach up to 100.\\nComparing the left and the right diagram, we notice that \
an $n^{\\\\mathrm{LCC}}$  comprising more than 90\\npercent of all agents is \
almost always obtained if the link formation between agents is not restricted \
by the tolerance parameter $\\\\epsilon$ (Figure~\\\\ref{fig: \
phaseDiagHighTension} right). \\nYet, if the link supersaturation $\\\\mu$ is \
low, i.e. if we are close to the percolation threshold, the network of the \
LCC is very sparse.\\nIt becomes more compact with increasing link saturation \
$\\\\mu$.\\n\\nIn Figure~\\\\ref{fig: phaseDiagHighTension} (right) for large \
$\\\\mu$\\nthe final state obtained for low and high $\\\\hat \\\\rho$ looks \
almost the same, however the process to reach it is very different.\\nFor low \
$\\\\hat\\\\rho$, we are in the regime of spinodal decomposition, where one \
connected component is formed from the very beginning, also shown in \
Figure~\\\\ref{fig:perc}.\\nFor high $\\\\hat \\\\rho$ we first observe the \
formation of smaller groups.\\nThe large compact group is formed by the \
\\\\emph{dissolution} of the smaller ones at a much longer time scale.\\nThis \
is clearly visible in Figure~\\\\ref{fig:time}, where we plot the growth of \
the LCC over time for the three different scenarios percolation, spinodal \
decomposition and nucleation.\\nBoth percolation and spinodal decomposition \
occur very fast, but for different reasons.\\nPercolation only considers the \
growth of the network, without an energy barrier or dissolution.\\nSpinodal \
decomposition, on the other hand, is driven by the very high link \
supersaturation, i.e. the energy barrier is negligible and dissolution does \
not play a role because groups formed immediately reach a supercritical size. \
 \\nNucleation is rather slow because groups of critical size are only formed \
by fluctuations. \\n\\n\\\\begin{figure}[htbp]  \
\\\\centering\\n\\\\includegraphics[width=0.45\\\\textwidth]{figures/\
Comparing_perc_ost_spinod/comparingSize.pdf}\\n\\\\caption{Fraction of the \
largest connected group, $\\\\nu=n^{\\\\mathrm{LCC}}/N$, over time for the \
three scenarios: percolation: $n^{LCC}_{P}$ ($\\\\mu_P/2$ = 3, $c_{P}$ = 0),  \
spinodal decomposition: $n^{LCC}_{S}$ ($\\\\mu_S/2$ = 19, $c_{S}$ = 0) and \
nucleation: $n^{LCC}_{O}$ ($\\\\mu_O/2$ = 19, $\\\\hat \\\\rho$ = 17.2). \
Parameters: $\\\\epsilon$ = 1, $\\\\gamma$ = 0.1, $\\\\beta$ = 0.4.\\n      }\
\\n  \\\\label{fig:time}\\n\\\\end{figure}\\n\\nTurning to the case of \
restricted link formation shown in Figure~\\\\ref{fig: \
phaseDiagHighTension}(left), we observe that a giant connected component is \
almost never observed because agents from different groups cannot create \
links if the tolerance value $\\\\epsilon$\\nis low, with only one exception \
that we explain now.\\n\\nFor the parameter constellation with  $(\\\\mu/2,\\\
\\hat \\\\rho$)= (20,4.8) we observe \\\\emph{coagulation}.\\nFor the same \
$\\\\mu$ this was not possible for smaller values of $\\\\hat \\\\rho$, \
because there large groups form quickly and the large group influence \
$\\\\alpha$ makes them quite homogeneous with respect to agents' \
opinions.\\nAs a consequence, these groups quickly reach average opinions too \
different for them to merge. \\nWith increasing $\\\\hat \\\\rho$, nucleation \
dominates.\\nMore smaller groups with more different average opinions form, \
which increases the chance for coagulation.\\nTherefore, we observe the \
formation of a giant connected component.\\n\\nFor the same $\\\\mu$, but \
even larger values of $\\\\hat \\\\rho$ the nucleation barrier rises and \
fewer groups are formed.\\nIf groups establish, they reach a homogeneous \
opinion very fast, because of the high group influences.\\nThis then does not \
allow free agents with different opinions to still join and hence the LCC \
becomes smaller, as we see. \\n\\n\\nFigure~\\\\ref{fig: \
phaseDiagHighTension}(left) also demonstrates that the final states of  \
spinodal decomposition and nucleation can be very different. \\nThe \
nucleation scenario allows groups to dissolve such that agents can join \
larger groups.\\nSpinodal decomposition, on the other hand, generates only a \
small incentive for agents to leave groups, because of the small value for \
$\\\\hat \\\\rho$.\\nTherefore, the groups formed coexist in a stable \
state.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\\
n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\"\>"}, {5,
  "5"->"\<\"{Analytical \
investigations}\\n\\\\label{sec:analyt-invest}\\n\\n\\n\\\\subsection{\
Incremental growth}\\n\\\\label{sec:crit-clust-size}\\n\\nEventually, we want \
to underpin our agent based simulations with some analytic results.\\nThese \
can be hardly obtained if we consider the full master \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:8}.\\nTherefore, we now discuss two limit cases, (i) \
incremental growth and (ii) densification.\\nThese can be seen as the two \
sides of the same coin, as they both occur during a phase transition, albeit \
dominate at different times. \\nConsidering them separately allows us to \
better understand the driving forces of group formation and competition. \
\\n\\nFirst, we omit processes of coagulation and densification and consider \
only incremental growth, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:21-inc}, and dissolution, \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:400}.\\nLet us further consider $\\\\epsilon$ = 1, i.e. all \
agents are allowed to form links.\\nThe respective growth process is \
illustrated in Figure~\\\\ref{fig:symbolic representation}(b).\\nPrecisely, a \
group can only grow if a new agent joins by connecting to the group with one \
link.\\nThis leads to a very sparse cluster where $m_{k}=n_{k}-1$, i.e. \
$\\\\rho_{k} = 2$ for large groups.\\nClearly, in this limit we do not need \
to discuss the dynamics of $\\\\rho_{k}$, we only focus on the size of the \
group, $n_{k}$. \\n\\nTo find out under which conditions it is more likely to \
observe the growth or the dissolution of a group, we calculate the logit or \
\\\\emph{odds ratio}, $G$, which is  defined as \\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n  \\\
\\label{eq:7}\\n  G = \\\\ln \
\\\\frac{w^{+}\\\\left[g_{k},g_{1}\\\\right]}{w^{-}\\\\left[ \
g_{k},\\\\rho_{k}\\\\right]}=\\n  \\\\ln \
\\\\left[\\\\frac{2m_{0}n_{0}n_{k}}{N^{2}} \
\\\\frac{N}{n_{k}\\\\exp\\\\{\\\\beta \
\\\\hat{\\\\rho}/\\\\rho_{k}\\\\}}\\\\right]\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nFrom $G=0$ \
we find  the critical conditions to expect incremental group \
growth:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:101}\\n  \
\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)=\\\\frac{\\\\beta\\\\hat{\\\\rho}}{\\\\ln\\\\\
left[2m_{0}(t)\\\\, n_{0}(t)/N \\\\right]}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nWe remind that \
$m_{0}(t)$ gives the number of links \\\\emph{available} at time $t$ and \
$n_{0}(t)$ the number of free agents, as defined in Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:1}. \
\\nIf we use $\\\\hat{\\\\rho}=2c/b$ and define a \
supersaturation\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \
y_{0}(t)=\\\\frac{2m_{0}(t)n_{0}(t)}{N}=n_{0}(t)\\\\mu_{0}(t) \\\\quad \
\\\\mathrm{with} \\\\quad\\n  \
\\\\mu_{0}(t)=\\\\frac{2m_{0}(t)}{M^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}}\\\\;;\\\\quad\\n    \
y_{0}(0)=N\\\\frac{M}{M^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}}\\\\equiv y\\n  \
\\\\label{eq:35}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nwe can rewrite the expression for the \
critical density very similar to the critical radius for nucleation, \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:31}:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:10}\\n  \
\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)\\n=\\\\frac{\\\\beta\\\\hat{\\\\rho}}{\\\\ln \
\\\\mu_{0}(t) + \\\\ln n_{0}(t) } =\\n  \\\\frac{2c}{b/\\\\beta} \\\\frac{1}{\
\\\\ln y_{0}(t)}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nwhere the cost $c$ takes the place of the \
surface tension. \\nWe emphasize that $\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$ does not \
make any statement about the \\\\emph{size} of a group.\\nIn fact it \
characterizes the system because it depends on the supersaturation $y_{0}(t)$ \
which is a system variable.\\n\\nOnly groups with \
$\\\\rho_{k}>\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$ are expected to grow incrementally \
in \\\\emph{size}, while groups with \
$\\\\rho_{k}<\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$ will dissolve. \\nOn the other \
hand, $\\\\rho_{k}=2$ for sparse groups. \\nHence, if we expect a stable \
group, the critical density $\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)$ should reach \
approximately 2 for large groups. \\nThis is indeed shown in \
Figure~\\\\ref{fig:inc-LCC-rho}, where we plot the density of the largest \
connected component.\\nInitially, $\\\\rho_{k}$ is smaller than 2 because of \
finite size effects.\\nFor instance, for $n_{k}=3$ we would have  \
$\\\\rho_{k}=2(n_{k}-1)/n_{k}=4/3<2$. \\nBut after the initial formation of a \
large group $\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{LCC}}$ approaches the value of 2, while \
$\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$ fluctuates around this value.\\nThe \
fluctuations result from the fact that the largest group continues to have \
spontaneous additions and dropouts of agents.\\nWe remind that \
$\\\\hat{\\\\rho}\\\\gg 2$ is compararably large, i.e. for agents the \
transition rate to leave the group is not negligible.\\nBecause both $n_{0}$ \
and $\\\\mu_{0}$ are small, changes in these numbers impact the critical \
density, $\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}_{n}$.\\nThe density of the group, \
$\\\\rho_{k}$, on the other hand, is not much impacted if a single agent \
joins or leaves because of the large group size. \
\\n\\n\\\\begin{figure}[htbp] \
\\\\centering\\n\\\\includegraphics[width=0.45\\\\textwidth]{figures/\
IncrementalGrowth/rho_crit.png}\\n   \\\\caption{Evolution of the density of \
the LCC and the critical density in the system over time. $\\\\mu/2$ = 20, \
$\\\\hat \\\\rho = 21.4$, $\\\\epsilon = 1$, $\\\\gamma = 0.1$, $\\\\beta$ = \
0.25, $N = 100$}\\n  \\\\label{fig:inc-LCC-rho}\\n\\\\end{figure}\\n\\nBy \
comparing Figures~\\\\ref{fig:time}, \\\\ref{fig:inc-LCC-rho}, we can \
estimate the time scales for the dynamics of $n_{k}(t)$ and    \
$\\\\rho_{k}(t)$.\\nObviously, the stable density $\\\\rho_{k}=2$ is reached  \
much faster, at about $t\\\\approx 0.1$. \\nBut this still allows the largest \
group to grow, i.e. $n_{k}(t)$ continues to change until $t\\\\approx \
10$.\\nTherefore, in the following we will analytically describe its \
dynamics.\\nIf we only consider incremental growth and dissolution processes \
for groups, Eqs.~\\\\eqref{eq:21-inc}, \\\\eqref{eq:400}, we can write down a \
dynamics for the expectation value of the group size $\\\\mean{n_{k}}=\\\\sum\
\\\\nolimits_{k^{\\\\prime}}n_{k^{\\\\prime}}P(n_{k^{\\\\prime}},t)$, by \
starting from:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:15}\\n  \
\\\\frac{d\\\\mean{n_{k}(t)}}{dt} &= \
\\\\mean{w^{+}\\\\left[g_{k},g_{1}\\\\right] - \
w^{-}\\\\left[g_{k},\\\\rho_{k}\\\\right]} \\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n           \
                   &= \\\\frac{\\\\mean{n_{k}(t)}}{N}\\\\left[ \
\\\\mean{y_{0}(t)} - e^{\\n                                \
\\\\beta\\\\frac{\\\\hat\\\\rho}{\\\\mean{\\\\rho_{k}(t)}}\\n                 \
               }\\\\right]\\n\\\\end{align}\\nIf we insert the derived \
expression for the critical density $\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)$, \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:10}, we can express the dynamcis as:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \
\\\\label{eq:28}\\n  \\\\frac{d \\\\ln \\\\mean{n_{k}(t)}}{dt} &=  \
\\\\frac{\\\\beta\\\\hat\\\\rho}{N} \\\\left[ \
\\\\frac{1}{\\\\mean{\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)}} - \
\\\\frac{1}{\\\\mean{\\\\rho_{k}(t)}}  \\\\right]                             \
   \\n\\\\end{align}\\nHere we have used a linear expansion of $\\\\exp(x)$ \
and $\\\\ln(x)$.\\nEqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:28} defines a \\\\emph{selection \
equation} \\\\citep{ebeling2011physics} that couples the growth of all groups \
via $\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$. \\nThe \\\\emph{size} of group $k$ grows \
as long as its density is larger than the critical density \
$\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$.\\nWe remind that a similar selection equation \
appears in nucleation theory, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:32}, where the coupling was \
given by the critical radius \
$r_{0}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)$.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\\
n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\\\subsection{Group \
densification}\\n\\\\label{sec:group-densification}\\n\\nWe now turn to the \
second limit case where instead of incremental growth only densification is \
considered, as shown in Figure~\\\\ref{fig:symbolic representation}(c) and \
described by Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:14}.\\nWe can then calculate the odds ratio \
as:\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\n  \\\\label{eq:700}\\n  G = \\\\ln \\\\frac{w^{+}\
\\\\left[g_{k},m_{0}\\\\right]}{w^{-}\\\\left[ \
g_{k},\\\\rho_{k}\\\\right]}=\\n  \\\\ln \\\\left[\\\\frac{2m_{0}n_{k}[n_{k}-\
\\\\rho_{k}]}{N^{2}} \\\\frac{N}{n_{k}\\\\exp\\\\{\\\\beta \
\\\\hat{\\\\rho}/\\\\rho_{k}\\\\}}\\\\right]\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nFrom $G=0$ \
we find the critical density for densification: \\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \
\\\\label{eq:101ng}\\n  \
\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}_{d}(t)=\\\\frac{\\\\beta\\\\hat{\\\\rho}}{\\\\ln \
\\\\mu_{0}(t) + \\\\ln \\\\omega_{k}(t)}\\n  \\\\;;\\\\quad \\\\omega_{k}(t)=\
\\\\left[n_{k}(t)-\\\\rho_{k}(t)\\\\right]\\n\\\\end{align}\\nWe note that \
the structure of $\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$ is similar to \
$\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:10}. \\n$\\\\omega_{k}(t)$ \
gives the number of ``free'' links \\\\emph{inside}  group $k$, while \
$n_{0}(t)$ which gives the number of free agents \\\\emph{outside} the group. \
\\nThat means, $\\\\omega_{k}(t)$ differs for groups in different \
configurations ($n_k, m_k$).\\n\\nHow does the additional critical density \
$\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$ influence the growth of groups?\\nWe recall \
that in the limit of incremental growth only $n_{k}$ changes according to \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:28}, while $\\\\rho_{k}$ remains constant. \\nIn the limit \
of densification, on the other hand, only the number of links $m_{k}$ inside \
a group changes, while $n_{k}$ remains constant.\\nTherefore, we need to \
develop a dynamics for $m_{k}$ now.\\n\\nThe dynamics for the expectation \
value of the number of links \
$\\\\mean{m_{k}}=\\\\sum\\\\nolimits_{k^{\\\\prime}}n_{k^{\\\\prime}}P(m_{k^{\
\\\\prime}},t)$, follows from:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:15m}\\n  \
\\\\frac{d\\\\mean{m_{k}(t)}}{dt} &= \
\\\\mean{w^{+}\\\\left[g_{k},m_{0}\\\\right] - \
w^{-}\\\\left[g_{k},\\\\rho_{k}\\\\right]} \\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n           \
                   &= \\\\frac{\\\\mean{n_{k}(t)}}{N}\\\\left[ \
\\\\mean{\\\\mu_{0}(t)\\\\omega_{k}(t)} - e^{\\n                              \
  \\\\beta\\\\frac{\\\\hat\\\\rho}{\\\\mean{\\\\rho_{k}(t)}}\\n               \
                 }\\\\right]\\n\\\\end{align}\\nIf we insert the derived \
expression for the critical density $\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)$, \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:101ng}, we can express the dynamcis \
as:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:28mk0}\\n  \\\\frac{d \
\\\\mean{m_{k}(t)}}{dt} &=  \
\\\\frac{\\\\beta\\\\hat\\\\rho}{N}\\\\mean{n_{k}(t)} \\\\left[ \
\\\\frac{1}{\\\\mean{\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)}} - \
\\\\frac{1}{\\\\mean{\\\\rho_{k}(t)}}  \\\\right]                             \
   \\n\\\\end{align}\\nwhich, in first order approximation \
$\\\\mean{\\\\rho_{k}}=2\\\\mean{m_{k}}/\\\\mean{n_{k}}$, eventually gives a \
selection equation for $\\\\mean{\\\\rho_{k}}$:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \
\\\\label{eq:28mk}\\n  \\\\frac{d \\\\mean{\\\\rho_{k}(t)}}{dt} &=  \\\\frac{\
\\\\beta\\\\hat\\\\rho}{N} \\\\left[ \
\\\\frac{1}{\\\\mean{\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)}} - \
\\\\frac{1}{\\\\mean{\\\\rho_{k}(t)}}  \\\\right]                             \
   \\n\\\\end{align}\\nWe note that the two critical densities for \
incremental growth and for densification are not independent of another, they \
are related via $\\\\mu_{0}(t)$.\\nBy solving Eqs. \
\\\\eqref{eq:10}\\\\eqref{eq:101ng} for $\\\\mu_{0}(t)$ we \
find:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:37}\\n  \
\\\\frac{\\\\beta\\\\hat\\\\rho}{\\\\mean{\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)}}=\\\
n  \\\\frac{\\\\beta\\\\hat\\\\rho}{\\\\mean{\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)}}\
 -\\\\ln{\\\\frac{\\\\omega_{k}}{n_{0}}}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nAfter the initial \
formation of a larger number of groups,\\nagents will leave groups with \
subcritical density, to join  larger groups with supercritical \
density.\\nThis increases the size of supercritical groups at the expense of \
subcritical groups which dissolve eventually.\\nThis reduces the number of \
groups and will be described analytically  in the \
following.\\n\\n\\\\subsection{Competition between \
groups}\\n\\\\label{sec:ostwald-ripening}\\n\\nFigure~\\\\ref{fig:group-dist}(\
a) shows the evolution of the group distribution for $N=500$, by plotting the \
quantity $n_{k}N_{n_{k}}$ over time.\\n$n_{k}$ is the size of group $k$ and \
$N_{n_{k}}$ is the number of groups with size $n_{k}$.\\nIn an early stage, \
we find a large number of smaller groups of subcritical size, \
$n_{k}<6$.\\nBecause $N$ is comparably small, only \\nfew of these groups \
reach a supercritical size.\\nOnly one continues to grow incrementally and \
via coagulation, while the subcritical groups either dissolve gradually or \
fragment. \\nFor larger systems, we find more supercritical groups, but this \
does not affect our principal \
discussion.\\n\\n\\nFigure~\\\\ref{fig:group-dist}(b) shows the total number \
of groups $K$ over time. \\nStarting from zero, in a very short time, $ \
t\\\\leq 2$, up to 30 groups of small size are formed\\nIn a second phase, $2 \
\\\\leq t \\\\leq 4$, this number decreases. \\nIn the end, we find one large \
group surrounded by a small number of free agents, as illustrated in \
Figure~\\\\ref{fig:nucleation}(c). \\n\\n\\\\begin{figure}[htbp]   \
\\\\centering\\n   \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.45\\\\textwidth]{figures/eps1_500Agents/\
groupSizeDist500.pdf}(a)\\n   \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.45\\\\textwidth]{figures/eps1_500Agents/K_\
fullModel_small.pdf}(b)\\n   \\\\caption{(a) Distribution of agents in \
$N_{n}$ groups of size $n$ (in $\\\\log$ scale) at various time steps.\\n     \
(b) Total number of groups, $K$, over time.\\n     Parameters: $N$ = 500, $\\\
\\mu/2$ = 5, $\\\\hat \\\\rho$ = 17.2, $\\\\epsilon$ = 1, $\\\\gamma$ = 0.1, \
$\\\\alpha$ = 0.9, $\\\\beta$ = 0.4.\\n   }\\n  \
\\\\label{fig:group-dist}\\n\\\\end{figure}\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nTo describe this \
evolution, we need to consider the dynamics for $n_{k}(t)$, \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:28}.\\nDensification does not change the size or the number \
of groups, but it can stabilize established groups. \\nThe smallest group \
size is $n_{k}=2$, otherwise agents are considered as free agents and counted \
in $n_{0}$.\\nHence if $n_{k}\\\\to 1$, the group has dissolved and $K(t)$ is \
diminished by 1.\\nAccording to Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:28}, $n_{k}$ decreases if $\
\\\\rho_{k}<\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$.\\nThis does not exclude processes \
to increase $\\\\rho_{k}$ via densification, therefore we base our further \
discussion on the selection Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:28} for $n_{k}(t)$. \\n\\nWe \
start from the conservation of agents, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:1}, which takes the \
sum over all existing groups:\\n$\\\\sum\\\\nolimits_{k}n_{k}=[N-n_{0}]$. \
\\nThis yields ${d n_{0}(t)}/{dt}= - \\\\sum\\\\nolimits_{k} {dn_{k}}/{dt}$. \
\\nLet us define the average group size $\\\\bar{n}$ and the average group \
density $\\\\bar{\\\\rho}$ as:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:36}\\n  \\\
\\bar{n}(t)= \\\\frac{1}{K(t)}{\\\\sum\\\\nolimits_{k} \
n_{k}(t)}=\\\\frac{1}{K(t)}[N-n_{0}]\\\\;;\\\\quad\\n  \\\\bar{\\\\rho}(t)= \
\\\\frac{1}{K(t)}{\\\\sum\\\\nolimits_{k} \
\\\\rho_{k}(t)}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nwhere $K(t)$ is the total number of groups \
at a given time.\\nFurther, we assume that in a first order approximation $\\\
\\sum\\\\nolimits_{k}{n_{k}}/{\\\\rho_{k}}=K \\\\bar{n}/\\\\bar{\\\\rho}$. \
\\nThen we obtain from Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:28}: \\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \
\\\\label{eq:12}\\n- \\\\frac{dn_{0}(t)}{dt}= & \\\\sum\\\\nolimits_{k} \
\\\\frac{\\\\beta \
\\\\hat\\\\rho}{N}n_{k}(t)\\\\left[\\\\frac{1}{\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)\
}-\\\\frac{1}{\\\\rho_{k}(t)}\\\\right] =   \\\\frac{\\\\beta \
\\\\hat\\\\rho}{N} K(t) \\\\bar{n}(t)  \
\\\\frac{1}{\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)}\\n      - \\\\frac{\\\\beta \
\\\\hat\\\\rho}{N} K(t) \
\\\\frac{\\\\bar{n}(t)}{\\\\bar{\\\\rho}(t)}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nwhich can be \
solved for $\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)$:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \
\\\\label{eq:29}\\n  \\\\frac{1}{\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)}=\\n  \
\\\\frac{1}{\\\\bar\\\\rho(t)}\\n  - \\\\frac{N}{\\\\beta\\\\hat \
\\\\rho}\\\\frac{1}{K(t)\\\\bar{n}(t)}\\\\frac{d \
n_{0}(t)}{dt}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nThis allows us to replace $1/\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\
\\mathrm{cr}}$ in Eqn. \\\\eqref{eq:28} such that we finally arrive \
at:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:25}\\n  \\\\frac{d \\\\ln \
n_{k}(t)}{dt}= \
\\\\frac{\\\\beta\\\\hat\\\\rho}{N}\\\\left[\\\\frac{1}{\\\\bar \
\\\\rho(t)}-\\\\frac{1}{\\\\rho_{k}(t)}\\\\right] \
-\\\\frac{1}{K(t)\\\\bar{n}(t)}\\\\frac{d n_{0}}{dt}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nEqn.~\
\\\\eqref{eq:25} has several advantages:\\n(i) Instead of the well defined, \
but unknown, variable $\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$, which characterizes the \
system via $y_{0}(t)$, we can use the average group density \
$\\\\bar{\\\\rho}$, which characterizes the groups.\\nNote that \
$\\\\bar{\\\\rho}$ represents all groups with subcritical and supercritical \
densities and  also reflects any densification of groups, i.e. it is the \
appropriate aggregated variable.\\n\\n(ii)  For the dynamics of \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:25} we can distinguish two stages of the phase \
transition.\\nIn the first stage of \\\\emph{group formation}, groups are \
small and of comparable density.\\nHence $\\\\rho_{k}\\\\approx \
\\\\bar{\\\\rho}$ and the first term vanishes. \\nTherefore the dynamics is \
dominated by the last term:   \\n $(1/K\\\\bar{n})({d n_{0}}/{dt})$ decreases \
rapidly because the number of free agents, $n_{0}$, decreases fast \
\\\\emph{and} the number of groups, $K$, as well as the average group size, $\
\\\\bar{n}$, grows.\\n In the second phase of \\\\emph{group competition} we \
can already assume that ${d n_{0}}/{dt}\\\\approx 0$.\\n Hence the dynamics \
is dominated by the first term in square brackets, which describe a \
\\\\emph{slow} selection process.  \\n\\n(iii) The meaning of competition and \
selection is made explicit in the dynamics:\\nThe growth of groups is coupled \
by the mean density $\\\\bar{\\\\rho}$. \\nBecause of ${d \
n_{0}}/{dt}\\\\approx 0$,  the growth of groups with a larger density can \
only occur because of the dissolution of groups with a smaller \
density.\\nThis process continues as long as $\\\\bar{\\\\rho}$ can still \
grow.\\nThe dynamics reaches a (theoretical) stationary state if only one \
group is left which defines the average density, \
$\\\\bar\\\\rho=\\\\rho_{k}$.\\nThis assumes that all agents can form links, \
which implies $\\\\epsilon=1$.\\nIf the tolerance threshold $\\\\epsilon$ is \
low, smaller groups will still dissolve.\\nBut the free agents cannot join \
the larger group because of the differences in opinions.\\nTherefore they \
remain as isolated agents, as shown in Figure~\\\\ref{fig:nucleation}(c). \\n\
\\n\\n\"\>"}, {6,
  "6"->"\<\"{Discussion}\\n\\\\label{sec:discussion-1}\\n\\n\\\\subsection{A \
coherent view on the dynamics of group \
formation}\\n\\\\label{sec:coher-view-dynam}\\n\\nWe have now all pieces \
together to present a coherent view of the group formation and competition, \
with particular emphasis on the nucleation scenario.\\nWe structure the \
discussion with respect to the various critical densities that are introduced \
in this paper. \\nThe first one is $\\\\hat\\\\rho=2c/b$, which is constant \
and defines the minimum density for a group to have a \\\\emph{non-negative \
utility}.\\nSocial agents evaluate costs $c$ and benefits of being part of a \
group $b$ and will leave if they do not gain a utility from this, \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:400}.\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{$\\\\hat\\\\rho<\\\\rho_{k}$.}\\\
nGroups need to have a density $\\\\rho_{k}>\\\\hat\\\\rho$ to remain or even \
grow, otherwise they will dissolve.\\nFor the percolation scenario $\\\\hat\\\
\\rho=0$, i.e. groups will never dissolve.\\nBut it needs a critical \
supersaturation $y>1$, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:35}, to obtain a large group that \
connects almost all agents.\\nYet, because $y$ is comparably small, the \
largest connected group is still very sparse, as Figure~\\\\ref{fig:perc}(c) \
shows.\\nFor the scenario of spinodal decomposition, the condition  \
$\\\\rho_{k}>\\\\hat\\\\rho$ is also fulfilled, because $\\\\hat \\\\rho$ is \
very small.\\nBut, different from percolation, the initial saturation $y$ is \
very large.\\nThus, we see the fast formation of one large compact \
group.\\n\\nThe differences can be also observed in Figure~\\\\ref{fig:qt} \
where we plot the density of the largest group over time.\\nPercolation \
occurs rather fast, but the largest group reaches only a small final density \
because it is a sparse network.\\nSpinodal decomposition occurs very fast \
because of the large initial instability.\\nBecause the initial \
supersaturation is high, i.e. a large number of links is potentially \
available, the final density of the largest group is also high. \
\\n\\n\\\\begin{figure}[htbp] \\\\centering\\n \\\\includegraphics[width=0.45\
\\\\textwidth]{figures/Comparing_perc_ost_spinod/comparingDensity.pdf}\\n \
\\\\caption{Density of the largest connected group over time for percolation, \
$\\\\rho^{LCC}_{P}$ ($\\\\mu_P$/2 = 3, $c_{P}$ = 0), spinodal decomposition, \
$\\\\rho^{LCC}_{S}$ ($\\\\mu_S$/2 = 19, $c_{S}$ = 0) and nucleation, \
$\\\\rho^{LCC}_{O}$ ($\\\\mu_O/$2 = 19, $\\\\hat \\\\rho$ = 17.2). \
Parameters: $\\\\epsilon$ = 1, $\\\\gamma$ = 0.1, $\\\\beta$ = 0.4.\\n }\\n  \
\\\\label{fig:qt}\\n\\\\end{figure}\\n\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{$\\\\rho_{k}<\\\\\
hat\\\\rho$.}\\nFor the nucleation scenario, the condition  \
$\\\\rho_{k}>\\\\hat\\\\rho$ is \\\\emph{never} fulfilled.\\nThat means, \
different from the other two scenarios, we will \\\\emph{always} observe that \
agents leave a group.\\nTherefore, even the largest group experiences \
fluctuations in size.\\nAs another consequence, groups can form initially \
only by means of fluctuations and have to overcome an energy barrier, \
quantified  by the critical density $\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$, \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:10} which \\n is plotted in \
Figure~\\\\ref{fig:500Agents-new}.\\n\\nHence, in this scenario it takes much \
longer before a large connected group can establish, which is also shown in \
Figure~\\\\ref{fig:qt}.\\nThe final density of the group is largely \
determined by the initial supersaturation, i.e. the number of potentially \
available links per agent.\\nThe large group is not as sparse as in the case \
of percolation, but not as dense as in the case of spinodal decomposition. \
\\n\\n\\\\begin{figure}[htbp] \\\\centering\\n   \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.45\\\\textwidth]{figures/eps1_500Agents/rho_nu_\
dens_500_4x3.pdf}(a)\\n   \
\\\\includegraphics[width=0.45\\\\textwidth]{figures/eps1_500Agents/rho_nu_\
dens_500_FULL4x3.pdf}(b)\\n   \\\\caption{Evolution of the density, \
$\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{LCC}}$, and the fraction, $\\\\nu$, of the largest \
connected group, the mean density, $\\\\bar\\\\rho$, the critical densities, \
$\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$, $\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$. (a) Early \
time period, (b) long term dynamics. \\n     Parameters: $N$ = 500, \
$\\\\mu/2$ = 5, $\\\\hat \\\\rho$ = 17.2, $\\\\epsilon$ = 1, $\\\\gamma$ = \
0.1, $\\\\alpha$ = 0.9, $\\\\beta$ = 0.4.\\n   }\\n  \
\\\\label{fig:500Agents-new}\\n\\\\end{figure}\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{$\\\\rho_{n}\
^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t) <\\\\rho_{k}< \\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)$.}\\n\\nIf \
 $\\\\rho_{k}<\\\\hat\\\\rho$, the two critical densities for growth, \
$\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:10}, and for densification, \
$\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:101ng}, come into \
play.\\nThese are plotted in Figure~\\\\ref{fig:500Agents-new}. \\n\\nAs we \
show in Figure \\\\ref{fig:500Agents-new}(a), during the initial stage of \
group formation, $t\\\\leq 2$, $\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)$ is very \
large.\\nThis implies that initially densification \\\\emph{cannot} take \
place because $\\\\rho_{k}(t)$ is too small.\\nAs a consequence, the \
dominating process in the initial stage is the incremental growth of \
$n_{k}(t)$.\\nWe see this in Figure \\\\ref{fig:500Agents-new}(a), where \
relative size of the largest group, $\\\\nu=n^{\\\\mathrm{LCC}}/N$ \
continuously increases, while its \\\\emph{density} has the constant value, $\
\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{LCC}}=2$, for incremental growth.  \\nAlso the critical \
density $\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$ is rather small, below 2, which allows \
the largest group to grow incrementally, according to the selection \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:28}. \
\\n\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{$\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t) < \
\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)<\\\\rho_{k}$.}\\nThe dynamics changes from \
incremental growth to densification once $\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)$ \
becomes small enough because the link supersaturation $\\\\mu_{0}(t)$ has \
decreased in the course of group formation.\\nPrecisely, once \
$\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}\\\\leq 2$, the process of densification starts \
to dominate .\\nThis does not mean that no group grows further.\\nIn fact, \
the largest group now grows \\\\emph{both} in size and in density, as shown \
in Figure \\\\ref{fig:500Agents-new}(a) because it fulfills the condition $\\\
\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t) < \\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)<\\\\rho_{k}$. \
\\n\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{$\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t) <\\\\rho_{k}< \
\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)$.}\\n\\nThe growth and densification of the \
largest group decreases the supersaturation $y_{0}=\\\\mu_{0}n_{0}$, hence \
$\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}_{n}$ increases considerably.\\nThis then stops the \
further \\\\emph{growth} of groups with $\\\\rho_{k}< \
\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)$.\\nThis does not exclude that these groups \
can densify, because $\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t) <\\\\rho_{k}$ \
holds.\\nBut the group \\\\emph{size} still diminishes because \
$\\\\rho_{k}<\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$,\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{$\\\\bar{\\\\\
rho}(t)\\\\approx\\\\rho_{k}$. }\\n\\nThe third relevant density is the \
average density $\\\\bar{\\\\rho}(t)$, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:36}.\\nAs an average \
over groups, $\\\\bar{\\\\rho}(t)$  describes the distribution of groups at a \
given time.\\nIn Figure~\\\\ref{fig:500Agents-new}(a) we observe that, for $t\
\\\\leq 2$,  $\\\\bar{\\\\rho}$ is almost constant, has a small value and \
fluctuates very little.\\nThis means that groups are small and of similar \
size and have not yet reached the density for sparse, but large clusters, $\\\
\\rho_{k}=2$. \\nThis observation further justifies why we have dropped, for \
the first stage, the first term of the competition Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:25}, \
because $\\\\rho_{k}\\\\approx \
\\\\bar{\\\\rho}$.\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{$\\\\bar{\\\\rho}(t)<\\\\rho^{\\\\\
mathrm{LCC}}$. }\\nFor the second stage, $2<t<4$, we observe instead a \
continuous growth of $\\\\bar\\\\rho(t)$ over time. \\nThis is mainly \
dominated by the density of the largest group, but fluctuates because small \
groups disappear.\\nWe further observe that for the largest group always \
$\\\\bar{\\\\rho}(t)<\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{LCC}}$ holds.\\nThat means, this \
group can grow further.\\nGroups with $\\\\rho_{k}<\\\\bar{\\\\rho}(t)$, on \
the other hand, cannot grow but will dissolve.\\nThis is reflected in the \
first term of the competition Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:25}, which now plays the \
mayor role in the \
dynamics.\\n\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{$\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}_{n}(t)$ vs. \
$\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}_{d}(t)$. }\\nFor the formation of new groups the \
relevant control parameter is $\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}_{n}(t)$.\\nIt \
determines the critical density that new groups need to reach if they want to \
establish. \\nWhereas $\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}_{d}(t)$ describes the \
evolution of the \\\\emph{group}, \\n$\\\\rho^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}_{n}(t)$ \
describes the evolution of the \\\\emph{system} because it depends on the \
supersaturation $y_{0}(t)$.\\nThis way, $\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)$ \
couples the growth of all groups via Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:28}.  \\nBecause \
$y_{0}(t)$ continuously decreases during the phase transition,  \
$\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)$ increases over time and sets a limit to the \
spontaneous group formation at a later time \
$t$.\\n\\n$\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$, on the other hand, is relevant only \
for the internal stability of the group during the second stage. \\nDuring \
the first stage densification does not play a role because \\n$\\\\rho_{k}<\\\
\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$.\\nDuring the second period densification becomes \
important, but only to some degree, because  \\nthe group \\\\emph{size} \
still diminishes as long as \
$\\\\rho_{d}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}<\\\\rho_{k}<\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}$.  \\n\
\\n\\n\\nFigure~\\\\ref{fig:500Agents-new}(b) shows the long-term dynamics of \
the process described.\\nIt allows to clearly separate the two stages: a \
\\\\emph{short initial period}, $t\\\\leq 2$ in which the formation of sparse \
groups dominates.\\nThis is followed by a \\\\emph{second stage} of about the \
same duration $2\\\\leq t\\\\leq 4$, which leads to the establishment of a \
large group.\\nThe precise values for the duration of course depends on the \
system size, for which we have chosen $N=500$ instead of $N=100$ from before. \
\\nDuring this second stage the number of groups reduces and the distribution \
of groups changes as plotted in Figure~\\\\ref{fig:group-dist}. \
\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\\\subsection{The structure of social \
groups}\\n\\\\label{sec:different}\\n\\nThe three different \
scenarios,\\npercolation, spinodal decomposition and nucleation, determine \
the network structure of groups, as detailed in the parameter plot of Figure~\
\\\\ref{fig: phaseDiagHighTension}(right).\\nThe above discussion has assumed \
that all agents can interact and form links unconditionally. \\nBut groups \
are also structured by the underlying social process of link formation, which \
we described by means of opinions, $x_{i}(t)$.\\nThese are internal variables \
of agents that determine whether two agents can interact, which in turn is \
the precondition of link formation and  group membership.\\nThese opinions \
are not only changed in bilateral interactions, they are further influenced \
by the group.\\nThat lead to the concept of an effective opinion that weights \
individual opinions against the average opinion of a group, using an \
additional parameter $\\\\alpha$ for group influence.\\n\\nAs we have \
demonstrated in Figure~\\\\ref{fig: phaseDiagHighTension}(left), restrictions \
in the interactions of agents, expressed by the tolerance threshold \
$\\\\epsilon$, can lead to very different macroscopic patterns.\\nInstead of \
one large connected group, we very often find that several  groups with \
\\ndifferent opinions finally coexist if $\\\\epsilon$ is small.\\nIn this \
case, the selection Eqs. \\\\eqref{eq:28}, \\\\eqref{eq:25} no longer \
hold.\\nAt least for the percolation case we are able to derive a relation, \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:3}, to determine whether a given initial link density \
$\\\\mu$ and a given tolerance threshold $\\\\epsilon$ would still allow for \
the formation of a large connected group. \\n\\nIf we take the social \
perspective then, different from physics, the final equilibrium state is less \
interesting than the dynamics that potentially lead to it.\\nSocial systems \
are non-equilibrium systems that adapt and evolve before an equilibrium is \
reached.\\nOur paper therefore investigates the \\\\emph{principal} ability \
of agents to form one large group, or to coexist in several separated groups.\
\\nA state where all agents are in the same group is rather unrealistic from \
a social perspective.\\nIn this respect, the nucleation scenario is the most \
promising one, as it leads to a distribution of groups that form \
spontaneously and compete for agents to grow.\\n\\nThe nucleation scenario \
also has the advantage to consider agents \\\\emph{leaving} the group if they \
experience a negative group utility.\\nSuch model features can bridge between \
our rather abstract approach and social processes of group \
formation.\\nAgents are allowed to constantly reevaluate their belonging to \
the social group.\\nWe could further consider that agents reevaluate their \
relations to those agents they are linked to.\\nThey could then delete a link \
if the difference between their current opinion and the opinions of their \
group members has reached a critical threshold \
\\\\citep{groeber_2014}.\\nAlso more complex decision rules to establish or \
to delete a link can be considered \\\\citep{Battiston2009,Centola2007}. \
\\n\\nAnother model feature with relevance to social systems is the explicit \
consideration of a \\\\emph{finite} number of agents and \
links.\\nThermodynamic models of phase transitions usually assume the limit \
of infinite systems.\\nWe instead address that social processes build on \
\\\\emph{limited} resources, be it available individuals or the ability to \
maintain social relations.\\nTherefore, we decided on purpose to present \
simulations with 100 or 500 agents. \\nThe depletion of these resources \
couples the dynamics of different groups on the systemic level, albeit in an \
indirect manner.\\nIt also decreases the chances for new groups to form at \
later stages.\\nHence, our model reflects the \\\\emph{first mover \
advantage}: groups that form early have a larger chance to reach a \
supercritical size or density.\\n\\nOur model of social group formation also \
considers that groups can \\\\emph{merge} or \\\\emph{split} into \
fractions.\\nThe merger process, which is called coagulation in a physical \
context, is an effective mechanism to overcome the critical group size \
because it increases the group size not incrementally, but in larger \
steps.\\nIn our model the possibility for mergers is principally restricted \
by the opinion dynamics.\\nIf we consider a strong group influence, all \
opinions inside a group quickly converge to their group average.\\nIf the \
tolerance threshold is low, groups with very different opinions can no longer \
merge, so they remain in coexistence, as nicely shown in \
Figure~\\\\ref{fig:thres-t}(c).\\nThis has indeed analogies to social or \
economic systems where \\\\emph{local cultures} \\\\citep{groeber_2009_2} \
inside groups or firms impact the success of mergers and acquisitions. \
\\n\\nFragmentation is not a separate process but follows from the \
dissolution of groups, Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:400}.\\nIf a link is removed, with a \
small probability the group breaks into pieces.\\nThis is more likely if \
groups have a small density.\\nSparse groups appear if two conditions are \
fulfilled: a rather low link supersaturation $\\\\mu$ close to the \
percolation threshold and a rather large $\\\\epsilon$, i.e. all links are \
accepted.\\nFor small $\\\\epsilon$, we obtain more compact groups because \
links within the group are more likely than between groups. \\n\\nEventually, \
we emphasize that one of the key variables of our model, the group \
\\\\emph{density} $\\\\rho_{k}$, is particularly relevant in a social \
context.\\nIn physical systems droplets are described as compact, \
homogeneous, spherical clusters with a radius $r_{k}$ and a fixed density $\\\
\\rho_{\\\\alpha}$, which is the same for all droplets.\\nSocial groups, on \
the other hand, are neither compact, nor spherical, nor homogeneous.\\nThey \
are like small social networks, more precisely disconnected components of a \
large social network.\\nTherefore they need to be characterized by two \
variables, the number of agents, $n_{k}$, and the number of links, $m_{k}$, \
which together define a time dependent group density.\\n$\\\\rho_{k}(t)$ in \
fact determines the \\\\emph{quality}, or the fitness, of a social group, \
which depends on the \\\\emph{relations} between group members rather than on \
the sheer number of members.\\nGiven a fixed group size, the number of \
realized relations makes all the difference for exchanging information, \
collaborating and sharing resources.\\nThis has been accounted for in our \
model by defining a group utility based on the group density.\\n\\nWhile \
being relevant in a social context, our model allows for analytic \
investigations to relate  the group density $\\\\rho_{k}$ to different \
critical densities, $\\\\hat{\\\\rho}$, $\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)$, \
$\\\\rho_{n}^{\\\\mathrm{cr}}(t)$ and $\\\\bar\\\\rho(t)$ which compress \
important information about the dynamics of social groups.\\nThis way, we \
could compactly describe the collective dynamics of all groups in a selection \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:25} that allows to separate early and late influences.\\nIn \
particular, it formally describes the \\\\emph{winner takes all} dynamics \
that is known from many social and economic systems \
\\\\citep{ebeling2011physics,Schweitzer2020}.\\n\\n\\n\\\\subsection{Outlook}\
\\n\\\\label{sec:outlook}\\n\\nAs a main conceptual contribution, in our \
paper we formally introduce  the \\\\emph{feedback} between the formation of \
social groups and the opinion dynamics of individuals.\\nAccording to the \
principle of homophily agents interact more if they are more similar with \
respect to some features.\\nWe capture these features in a rather abstract \
notion of ``opinion''.\\nAgents will establish social relations, i.e. links, \
if their similarity in opinions allows them to interact.\\nGroups are formed \
based on these social relations.\\nOnce they are established, they continue \
to influence the opinion of agents, this way impacting the possibility of \
agents to form new links.\\nTherefore, in our model we observe the \
\\\\emph{coevolution} between social group structures and \
opinions.\\nDifferent from simple models of network formation, the \
probability that two agents form a link is no longer a global and constant \
parameter, but (i) depends on the agents, and (ii) evolves over time.\\n\\nIn \
this paper, we have used only a simplified characterization of agents by \
means of a continuous scalar variable, $x_{i}(t)$.\\nIn a next step, we will \
extend our model by considering \\\\emph{multidimensional} opinions \
\\\\citep{schweighofer_2020}.\\nThis is not just an upscaling of the current \
model, but will in fact  allow for more diverse group configurations and for \
new system states.\\nThe multidimensional representation reflects the opinion \
of a single agent with respect to different issues.\\nTwo agents can have \
similar opinions about one particular issue, but vastly deviating ones about \
another issue.\\nThis then leads to the interesting question how agents \
settle their relations in such cases.\\nHere psychological concepts like \
dissonance minimization \\\\citep{festinger_1957,groeber_2014} or \
\\nstructural balance theory \\\\citep{heider1946attitudes, \
flache2017,schweighofer_2020_2,gorski2020homophily,Agbanusi2018,antal3, \
Kulakowski2019} come into play.\\nWith respect to social group formation, \
such conflicting situations would allow  agents to form a social group with a \
focus only on the one issue they agree on, for example a movement for \
environmental protection.\\nBut regarding another issue, agents would join a \
different group.\\n\\nTo model these more complex situations, we will explore \
a multilayer network representation \
\\\\citep{hu2011percolation,murase2014multilayer,Kivela2014,Scholtes2016,\\n  \
Atkisson2019,\\n  Gorski2017}.\\nThe nodes in each layer, i.e. the agents, \
are identical, but their relations in each layer can be different because \
\\neach layer contains information about one particular issue \
only.\\nDecoupled layers would display a dynamics of group formation similar \
to the one discussed in this paper.\\nHowever, in addition to the \
\\\\emph{intralayer} dynamics, we have to consider the \\\\emph{interlayer} \
dynamics of coupled layers.\\nThat is, how do the social dynamics of groups \
and opinions on one layer impact the dynamics on other layers?\\nCombining \
multilayer network models with models of group formation and opinion  \
dynamics will allow us to address long standing questions about \
\\\\emph{issue alignment} \\\\citep{schweighofer_2020}, i.e. how opinions on \
different issues influence another, on the emergence of social movements, or \
on conflict resolution and polarization of opinions in a novel \
manner.\\n\\n\\\\subsection*{Acknowledgements}\\n\\\\label{sec:\
acknowledgements}\\n\\nF.S. gratefully acknowledges early discussions on the \
dynamics of phase transitions in finite systems with four colleagues, who \
celebrate their milestone birthday in 2021: Heinz Ulbricht (90), Werner \
Ebeling (85), Gerd R\[CapitalATilde]\[Paragraph]pke (80), J\[CapitalATilde]\
\.bcrn Schmelzer (70). Their scientific advice has inspired the current \
manuscript. \\n\\n\\\\begin{appendix}\\n  \\n\"\>"}, {7,
  "7"->"\<\"*{Appendix A}\\n\\nTable \\\\ref{tab: summary parameters} below \
summarizes the correspondence between the mechanisms incorporated in our \
model, their rationale and their social relevance:\\n\\n \
\\\\renewcommand{\\\\arraystretch}{2}\\n\\\\begin{table}[httb]\\n\\\\begin{\
center}\\\\footnotesize\\n  \\\\begin{tabular}{@{} p{2.4cm}  p{3cm}  p{3cm}  \
p{3cm}  p{3cm} @{}} \\\\toprule\\n & Opinion formation & Group influence & \
Group formation & Network formation  \\\\\\\\[5pt]\\n  \\\\midrule\\nSocial \
relevance & \\\\makecell[l]{- social influence ($\\\\gamma$) \\\\\\\\ - \
tolerance ($\\\\epsilon$) \\\\\\\\\\n    }&\\n      \\\\makecell[l]{- local \
cultures ($\\\\alpha$) \\\\\\\\ - social norms ($\\\\alpha$)\\n        }&\\n  \
     \\\\makecell[l]{- communication ($b$) \\\\\\\\\\n    - maintain group \
($c$)} &  \\\\makecell[l]{- randomness ($\\\\beta$) \\\\\\\\\\n    - Dunbar's \
\\\\# ($M$)\\n    }\\\\\\\\[5pt]\\nRationale & \\\\makecell[l]{- restrict \
