\\\\\\\\[5pt]\\n\\n    Parameters & $\\\\gamma$  ,  $\\\\epsilon$ & \
$\\\\alpha$ & $(c,b)$ or $\\\\hat\\\\rho$ & $\\\\beta$  ,  \
$(N,M)$\\\\\\\\[5pt]\\n    \\\\bottomrule\\n  \
\\\\end{tabular}\\n\\\\end{center}\\n\\\\caption{Summary of model components}\
\\n\\\\label{tab: summary parameters}\\n\\\\end{table}\\n\"\>"}, {8,
  "8"->"\<\"*{Appendix B}\\n\\n\\n\\nIn the following we summarize the master \
equation formalism for considering the group distribution.\\nThe discussion \
is restricted to \\nthe case of $\\\\epsilon = 1$ and incremental growth and \
dissolution as the only possible transitions.\\n\\nThis perspective is based \
on $P[\\\\pmb{N},t]$, the probability to find the system at time $t$ with a \
group configuration $\\\\pmb{N}$.\\nHere, \
$\\\\pmb{N}=[N_{1,0},N_{2,1},N_{3,2},N_{3,3},...,N_{N,M}]$ is a vector with \
elements $N_{n,m}$ counting the number of groups of size $n$ with $m$ \
links.\\nWith this, the total number of links and agents,  \
Eqn.~\\\\eqref{eq:1}, can be rewritten as: \\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \
\\\\label{eq:A1}\\n  M=m_{0}(t) +\\\\sum_{n,m}N_{n,m}(t)m\\\\;;\\\\quad\\n   \
N=n_{0}(t) +\\\\sum_{n,m}N_{n,m}(t)n\\n\\\\end{align}\\nwhere \
$n_{0}(t)=N_{1,0}(t)$ is the number of free agents as defined in the main \
text.\\nThe transition rates for growth can then be written \
as:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{A3}\\nw^{+}_{n,m}[N_{1,0} N_{n,m}] \
\\\\propto \\\\frac{2 m_{0}}{N^{2}} (nN_{n,m})n_{0}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nand \
the transition rates for spontaneous leaving  read: \\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \
\\\\label{eq:A4}\\nw^{-}_{n,m}[N_{n,m}] \\\\propto \\\\frac{nN_{n,m}}{N}  \
\\\\exp{\\\\left\\\\{\\\\beta\\\\frac{\\\\hat{\\\\rho}}{\\\\rho}\\\\right\\\\}\
}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nUsing the above defined transition rates, we can write \
down the master equation for changes in the group \
distribution:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:8}\\n  \\\\frac{\\\\partial \
P(\\\\pmb{N},t)}{\\\\partial t}=& \\\\sum\\\\limits_{\\\\pmb{N^{\\\\prime}}\\\
\\neq \\\\pmb{N}} \
w[\\\\pmb{N}|\\\\pmb{N^{\\\\prime}}]P(\\\\pmb{N^{\\\\prime}},t) -\\n          \
                                  w[\\\\pmb{N^{\\\\prime}}|\\\\pmb{N}]P(\\\\\
pmb{N},t)\\n\\\\end{align}\\nwhich in explicit form can be written as \
follows:\\n\\\\begin{multline}\\n  \\\\label{eq:8}\\n  \\\\frac{\\\\partial \
P(N_{1,0},N_{2,1},N_{3,2},N_{3,3}...,t)}{\\\\partial t}=  \
w^{-}_{2,1}[N_{2,1}+1]P(N_{1,0}-2,N_{2,1}+1...,t) - \\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n  \
-  w^{+}_{1,0}[N_{1,0}]P(N_{1,0},N_{2,1}...,t) \\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n  +  \
w^{-}_{3,2}[N_{3,2}+1]P(N_{1,0}-1,N_{2,1}-1,N_{3,2}+1...,t) \\\\nonumber \\\\\
\\\\\\n  +  w^{+}_{1,0}[N_{1,0}+2]P(N_{1,0}+2, N_{2,1} -1, N_{3,2}...,t) \
\\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n  - (w^{+}_{2,1}[N_{1,0},N_{2,1}] + \
w^{-}_{2,1}[N_{2,1}])P(N_{1,0},N_{2,1}...,t) \\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n  +  \
\\\\sum_{n=3}^{N}\\\\{w^{-}_{n+1,n}[N_{n+1,n}+1]P(N_{1,0} -1,...,N_{n,n-1}+1, \
N_{n+1,n}+1...,t)\\\\} \\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n  +  \
w^{+}_{n-1,n-2}[N_{1,0}+1,N_{n-1,n-2}+1]P(N_{1,0}+1,...,N_{n-1,n-2}+1,N_{n,n-\
1}-1...,t) \\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n  -  (w^{+}_{n,n-1}[N_{1,0},N_{n,n-1}] + \
w^{-}_{n,n-1}[N_{n,n-1}])P(N_{1,0},...,t)\\n\\\\end{multline}\\nThe mean \
number of groups of size $(n,n-1)$ in case of incremental growth follows \
from: \\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\mean{N_{n,n-1}(t)} = \
\\\\sum_{\\\\pmb{N}_{i}} \
N_{n,n-1}P(\\\\pmb{N}_{i},t)\\n\\\\end{align}\\nwhere $\\\\pmb{N}_{i}$ refers \
to group distributions which have a total number of $N$ agents.\\nUsing the \
master equation and the transition probabilities, we can write for the \
time-dependent change:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:2}\\n  \
\\\\frac{d\\\\mean{N_{n,n-1}}}{dt} = \\\\mean{-w^{-}_{n,n-1}(N_{n,n-1}) - \
w^{+}_{n,n-1}(n_{0}N_{n}) + w^{-}_{n+1,n}(N_{n+1,n}) + \
w^{+}_{n-1,n-2}(n_{0}N_{n-1,n})}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nThis equation holds only \
for $n\\\\geq 2$.\\nFor $n=1$ we have to take into account both the \
probability for any group to shrink and grow, as well as the probability for \
two agents to form a group or break apart. We refrain from writing out this \
longer equation here. Also, we will drop the arguments of the transition \
rates to lighten the notation.\\n\\nBy taking a Taylor expansion for the \
transition rates, the following Fokker-Plank equation (only dependent on \
$N_{n,n-1}$ and $n_{0}$ now) can be found \\\\citep{Book-Ulbricht1988}: \\n\\\
\\begin{equation}\\n  \\\\frac{d\\\\mean{N_{n,n-1}}}{dt} = - \
\\\\mean{\\\\frac{\\\\partial }{\\\\partial n}\\\\left\\\\{ w^{+}_{n,n-1} - \
w^{-}_{n} \\\\right\\\\} } + \\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\mean{\\\\frac{\\\\partial^{2} \
}{\\\\partial n^{2}}\\\\left\\\\{ w^{+}_{n,n-1} + w^{-}_{n} \\\\right\\\\} \
}\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nWe only consider the first part of this equation, \
neglecting  fluctuations in the system.\\nUsing our transition rates and \
assuming $\\\\mean{N_{n,n-1}n_{0}} = \\\\mean{N_{n,n-1}}\\\\mean{n_{0}}$, the \
first part can be written as follows:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \
\\\\label{eq:6}\\n  \\\\frac{d \\\\mean{N_{n,n-1}}}{dt} = - \
\\\\frac{\\\\partial }{\\\\partial n}  \\\\left\\\\{\\\\frac{2m_{0}}{N^{2}}\\\
\\mean{n}\\\\mean{N_{n,n-1}}\\\\mean{n_{0}} - \
\\\\frac{\\\\mean{n}\\\\mean{N_{n,n-1}}}{N}\\\\exp{\\\\beta \\\\frac{\\\\hat{\
\\\\rho}}{\\\\rho}}\\\\right\\\\}\\n\\\\end{align}\\nThis can be transformed \
into a continuity equation, which  describes the in- and outflow of agents \
into the different groups:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\\\label{eq:4}\\n  \\\\frac{d \
\\\\mean{N_{n,n-1}}}{dt} + \\\\nabla \\\\cdot [\\\\mean{N_{n,n-1}(t)}\\\\cdot \
\\\\dot{n}] = 0\\n\\\\end{align}\\nThis finally leads to the change in \
average group size $n$ which is discussed in the main part of the article \
already:\\n\\\\begin{align}\\n  \\\\label{eq:9}\\n  \\\\frac{d \
\\\\mean{n}}{dt} &= \\\\frac{2m_{0}}{N^{2}}\\\\mean{n}\\\\mean{n_{0}} - \
\\\\frac{\\\\mean{n}}{N}\\\\exp{\\\\beta \\\\frac{\\\\hat{\\\\rho}}{\\\\rho}}\
\\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n                        &= \
\\\\frac{\\\\mean{y_{0}(t)}\\\\mean{n}}{N} - \\\\frac{\\\\mean{n}}{N}\\\\exp{\
\\\\beta \\\\frac{\\\\hat{\\\\rho}}{\\\\rho}}\\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n  &=  \\\
